(window.SLV_PACKS = window.SLV_PACKS || []).push({"id":"fire","name":"Fire alarm","blurb":"Fire alarm, NFPA 72 based, small to mid-size commercial","calculators":["firebatt","nac","gauge"],"openTips":9,"draft":false,"verifyOpen":0,"learn":{"modules":[{"num":1,"title":"Fire Alarm Foundations","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> explain why fire alarm work is held to a higher standard than burglary work, name the codes that govern it, recognize power-limited fire alarm wiring, and follow the safety steps that come before touching a live fire system.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout\"><p>These lessons give an overview based on NFPA 72. Codes are adopted and amended locally, and editions change. Always confirm requirements with your company, the AHJ, the adopted code edition, and the equipment's installation manual.</p></aside>","lessons":[{"id":"1.1","title":"Why fire alarm is different","html":"<p>A burglar alarm protects property. A fire alarm protects <strong>lives</strong>. That one difference changes how the work is done:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>The rules are mandatory.</strong> Most commercial fire alarm systems are required by the building or fire code, designed to NFPA 72, and inspected by the fire marshal before anyone moves in.</li><li><strong>The design is engineered.</strong> Device locations, candela ratings, and wire sizes come from approved drawings. You install what's on the drawings; if something won't work in the field, you get it changed through the designer, not on your own.</li><li><strong>Every change is documented.</strong> Drawings, battery and voltage drop calculations, and test records follow the system for its whole life.</li><li><strong>The system must always be watching.</strong> Any time you take a fire system out of service, people in that building lose protection. You notify the right people before, and you put it back the way you found it after.</li><li><strong>Liability is real.</strong> If a system fails in a fire, the install and every service ticket will be examined.</li></ul>\n<p>Many states require a <strong>fire alarm license</strong> for the company and the technician, and many AHJs and employers require <strong>NICET certification</strong> in Fire Alarm Systems for designers and lead techs. Know what your state requires before you work on fire.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"1.2","title":"The codes and who enforces them","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Code or standard</th><th>What it covers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>NFPA 72</strong> (National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code)</td><td>How fire alarm systems are designed, installed, tested, and maintained</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NFPA 70</strong> (National Electrical Code), <strong>Article 760</strong></td><td>Fire alarm wiring: circuit types, cable ratings, separation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>IBC / IFC</strong> (International Building and Fire Codes) or <strong>NFPA 101</strong> (Life Safety Code)</td><td><em>When</em> a fire alarm is required, and what kind, based on building use and size</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NFPA 90A</strong></td><td>HVAC systems, including where duct smoke detectors are required</td></tr><tr><td><strong>UL 864</strong></td><td>The listing standard for fire alarm control units <span class=\"src\">(UL 864)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>The building code says whether you need a system. NFPA 72 says how to build it.</strong></p>\n<p>The <strong>AHJ</strong> (Authority Having Jurisdiction) is usually the fire marshal or fire prevention office. The AHJ:</p>\n<ul><li>Adopts a specific edition of each code, often with local amendments</li><li>Reviews and approves the drawings before installation (plan review)</li><li>Witnesses the acceptance test before the building is occupied</li><li>Can require more than the code minimum</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Listed equipment only.</strong> Every device, panel, and appliance must be listed for fire alarm use and <strong>listed as compatible</strong> with the panel it connects to. A smoke detector that works fine on one brand of panel may not be listed for another.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"1.3","title":"Power-limited fire alarm circuits and cable","html":"<p>Most of what you'll wire is <strong>power-limited fire alarm (PLFA)</strong> circuits: initiating device circuits, signaling line circuits, and notification appliance circuits from a listed fire alarm control unit. The panel limits the power on those circuits, so the NEC allows lighter wiring methods than for 120 V power.</p>\n<p><strong>Fire alarm cable types (NEC Article 760)</strong></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Marking</th><th>Where it's used</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>FPL</strong></td><td>General use, not in risers or plenums</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FPLR</strong></td><td>Riser: vertical runs between floors (also OK where FPL is allowed)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>FPLP</strong></td><td>Plenum: air-handling spaces above drop ceilings (OK anywhere)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>CI</strong> (circuit integrity)</td><td>Survivability: keeps working for a rated time in a fire</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rules to remember:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Fire alarm circuits are identified</strong> at terminal and junction locations so nobody mistakes them for something else and cuts power to them. Red box covers and red cable are common ways to do this, but red itself isn't required. <span class=\"src\">(NEC 760.30)</span></li><li><strong>Keep PLFA cables separated</strong> from power, lighting, and non-power-limited circuits. Don't share a box or raceway with 120 V wiring.</li><li><strong>Use the jacket rating for the space.</strong> Plenum spaces need FPLP (or cable in a raceway as allowed by the NEC and AHJ).</li><li><strong>Support the cable</strong> from the building structure, not from ceiling grid wires or pipes.</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"1.4","title":"Safety before you touch a fire system","html":"<p>Working on a live fire system can trigger an evacuation, dispatch the fire department, shut down HVAC, recall elevators, release doors, or <strong>discharge a suppression system</strong>. Before you do anything that could cause a signal:</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Call the monitoring center</strong> and put the account on test. Get the operator's name. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li><strong>Notify the owner or building contact</strong>, plus occupants if appliances will sound. Some jurisdictions also require notifying the fire department. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li><strong>Disable outputs you don't want to operate</strong> using the panel's disable or bypass functions, per the panel manual and the building's procedure: releasing circuits (clean agent, preaction, deluge), elevator recall, HVAC shutdown, door unlocking. <strong>Never test with a releasing circuit armed</strong> unless the test plan calls for a full discharge test with everyone involved. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li><strong>When you're done</strong>, re-enable everything you disabled, reset the panel, confirm it's normal with no troubles, and take the account off test with the monitoring center.</li></ol>\n<p>Other hazards on fire jobs:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>120 VAC is in the panel</strong> (and in power supplies and NAC extenders). Turn off and lock the dedicated breaker before working on primary power.</li><li><strong>Batteries can deliver very high current.</strong> Disconnect them before working in the panel; don't let a wrench or tool bridge the terminals.</li><li><strong>Ladders and lifts:</strong> most devices are on the ceiling. Use the right ladder, and a lift for high ceilings.</li><li><strong>If the system will be out of service</strong> for a while, the AHJ may require a <strong>fire watch</strong> (<a href=\"#/fire/learn/8\">Module 8</a>).</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the step new techs skip most often when putting a system on test, and what happens when they do.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"1.5","title":"Who's who on a fire alarm job","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>What they do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Owner</strong></td><td>Responsible for keeping the system inspected, tested, and maintained</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Designer / engineer</strong></td><td>Produces the drawings, calculations, and sequence of operations</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Installing contractor</strong></td><td>Pulls wire, mounts devices, terminates, programs (often you)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>AHJ / fire inspector</strong></td><td>Approves plans, witnesses acceptance tests, enforces the code</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Monitoring center</strong></td><td>Receives alarm, supervisory, and trouble signals and dispatches</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Sprinkler contractor</strong></td><td>Installs waterflow and valve tamper switches you'll monitor</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Elevator, HVAC, door hardware contractors</strong></td><td>Their equipment is controlled by the fire alarm; coordinate testing with them</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A smooth fire job is mostly coordination. Know who you need on site for the final test before you schedule it.</p>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["What's the biggest difference between what a burglar alarm and a fire alarm protect?","Which code says <em>how</em> to design and install a fire alarm system?","Which NEC article covers fire alarm wiring?","Which cable type is required in a plenum space above a drop ceiling?","What does &quot;listed as compatible&quot; mean for a smoke detector?","Name three things you do before testing a fire alarm system.","Why must releasing circuits be disabled before testing?","Who usually witnesses the acceptance test before a building is occupied?"],"answers":["A fire alarm protects lives; a burglar alarm protects property","NFPA 72","Article 760","FPLP (or cable in a raceway where the NEC and AHJ allow it)","The detector is listed to work with that specific control panel","Put the account on test with the monitoring center, notify the building, and disable outputs such as releasing, elevator recall, and HVAC shutdown","So a test signal doesn't discharge a suppression system","The AHJ (fire inspector)"],"keyHtml":"1) A fire alarm protects lives; a burglar alarm protects property. 2) NFPA 72. 3) Article 760. 4) FPLP (or cable in a raceway where the NEC and AHJ allow it). 5) The detector is listed to work with that specific control panel. 6) Put the account on test with the monitoring center, notify the building, and disable outputs such as releasing, elevator recall, and HVAC shutdown. 7) So a test signal doesn't discharge a suppression system. 8) The AHJ (fire inspector).","noteHtml":""}},{"num":2,"title":"How a Fire Alarm System Works","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> name the parts of a fire alarm system, tell the three signal types apart, explain conventional vs addressable systems, and trace a signal from a detector to the fire department.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"2.1","title":"The parts of a system","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fire alarm control unit (FACU / FACP)</strong></td><td>The brain. Watches every circuit, decides what each signal means, sounds the alarms, runs the control functions, and reports off site</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Initiating devices</strong></td><td>Inputs: smoke and heat detectors, manual pull stations, waterflow switches, valve tamper switches, duct detectors</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Notification appliances</strong></td><td>Outputs that warn people: horns, strobes, horn/strobes, speakers, chimes</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Annunciator</strong></td><td>A remote display (often at the main entrance) so firefighters can see what's in alarm without going to the panel</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Power</strong></td><td>Primary (a dedicated 120 VAC branch circuit) and secondary (batteries, sometimes a generator)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Communicator</strong></td><td>Sends signals to the monitoring center (DACT phone lines, cellular, IP, or radio)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Control outputs</strong></td><td>Relays and modules that recall elevators, shut down fans, release door holders, unlock doors, and trigger other systems</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NAC power extenders</strong></td><td>Booster power supplies that add notification circuits and power when the panel's own NAC capacity runs out</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","mins":2},{"id":"2.2","title":"The three signal types","html":"<p>Every condition a fire panel reports falls into one of three types. Knowing which one you're looking at tells you how urgent it is and who needs to know.</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Means</th><th>Examples</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Alarm</strong></td><td>A fire may be happening</td><td>Smoke detector, heat detector, pull station, waterflow</td><td>Notification appliances sound, control functions run, monitoring center dispatches the fire department</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Supervisory</strong></td><td>Something that protects the building is off-normal</td><td>Sprinkler valve closed (tamper), low air on a dry-pipe system, duct detector (in many systems), fire pump trouble</td><td>Panel sounds and shows supervisory; monitoring center calls the building to investigate. No evacuation</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trouble</strong></td><td>The fire alarm system itself has a fault</td><td>Open or ground on a circuit, AC power loss, low battery, missing device, communication failure</td><td>Panel sounds and shows trouble; monitoring center notifies the service company</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Some systems also have a <strong>pre-alarm</strong> (an addressable smoke detector getting close to its alarm threshold) that alerts staff before a full alarm.</p>\n<p><strong>Alarm signals take priority</strong> over supervisory, and supervisory over trouble.</p>\n<p><strong>Latching:</strong> alarm and supervisory signals stay on the panel until someone resets it, even after the device restores. Most troubles clear on their own when the fault is fixed.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"2.3","title":"Conventional vs addressable","html":"<h4>Conventional (zoned) systems</h4>\n<p>Detectors are wired on <strong>initiating device circuits (IDCs)</strong>, one circuit per zone, each supervised by an <strong>end-of-line (EOL)</strong> device at the far end. The panel only knows that <em>something on Zone 3</em> is in alarm, so someone has to go find which detector.</p>\n<p>Common in small buildings. Simple and inexpensive, but finding a problem means walking the zone.</p>\n<h4>Addressable (intelligent) systems</h4>\n<p>Every device has its own <strong>address</strong> and talks to the panel over a <strong>signaling line circuit (SLC)</strong>, a two-wire data loop. The panel knows exactly which device is in alarm or trouble: <em>&quot;Smoke detector, 2nd floor corridor outside room 214.&quot;</em></p>\n<p>Addressable systems also:</p>\n<ul><li>Report each detector's sensitivity and dirtiness</li><li>Let you disable one device instead of a whole zone</li><li>Use <strong>monitor modules</strong> to bring conventional devices (waterflow switches, tampers, older detectors) onto the SLC</li><li>Use <strong>control/relay modules</strong> to run outputs from anywhere on the loop</li></ul>\n<h4>Notification circuits</h4>\n<p>Either type uses <strong>notification appliance circuits (NACs)</strong> to power horns and strobes, usually at a regulated 24 VDC. The panel reverses polarity on the NAC to sound the appliances, so NAC appliances are <strong>polarized</strong> and use diodes or EOL resistors for supervision.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"2.4","title":"The signal path","html":"<ol><li><strong>Detection:</strong> a smoke detector senses smoke and sends an alarm (current draw on a conventional IDC, a data message on an addressable SLC).</li><li><strong>Panel processing:</strong> the panel confirms the signal (some smoke systems use <strong>alarm verification</strong>, which resets and rechecks the detector before going into alarm), then follows its programming.</li><li><strong>Occupant notification:</strong> NACs activate; horns sound the <strong>temporal-3</strong> evacuation pattern and strobes flash (<a href=\"#/fire/learn/4\">Module 4</a>).</li><li><strong>Control functions:</strong> elevators recall, fans shut down, door holders release, according to the <strong>sequence of operations</strong>.</li><li><strong>Off-site reporting:</strong> the communicator transmits to the monitoring center, which dispatches the fire department.</li><li><strong>Annunciation:</strong> the panel and annunciator display the device and location so the responding firefighters can go straight to it.</li></ol>\n<p>Silencing the horns does <strong>not</strong> reset the system. The panel stays in alarm until the cause is cleared and the panel is reset.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"2.5","title":"Monitoring and communication","html":"<p>Most commercial fire systems are monitored by a <strong>supervising station</strong>: a central station, a proprietary station (owned by the building), or a remote station.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>DACT</strong> (digital alarm communicator transmitter): sends signals over phone lines. Traditionally needs <strong>two</strong> separate communication paths.</li><li><strong>Cellular and IP communicators:</strong> increasingly the primary path as copper phone lines disappear. Many single-path cellular or IP communicators are now listed as an acceptable sole means when the path itself is supervised. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 26)</span></li><li><strong>Radio:</strong> private radio networks in some areas.</li></ul>\n<p>The panel must <strong>supervise its communication path</strong> and report a trouble if it can't get through. Test signals go to the monitoring center on a regular schedule (commonly at least every 24 hours) so a dead path gets noticed. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 26)</span></p>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["What's the difference between an initiating device and a notification appliance?","A sprinkler control valve is closed. Which signal type is that?","The panel loses AC power. Which signal type is that?","On a conventional system, what does the panel know when a detector goes into alarm?","What does an addressable system use to bring a waterflow switch onto the SLC?","Why are NAC appliances polarized?","Does silencing the horns reset the panel?","What does the annunciator do for firefighters?"],"answers":["Initiating devices detect and send signals in; notification appliances warn occupants","Supervisory","Trouble","Only which zone (circuit) is in alarm","A monitor module","The panel reverses polarity to sound them, and diodes or EOL resistors supervise the circuit in standby","No; the panel stays in alarm until it's reset","Shows which device and location is in alarm, away from the panel"],"keyHtml":"1) Initiating devices detect and send signals in; notification appliances warn occupants. 2) Supervisory. 3) Trouble. 4) Only which zone (circuit) is in alarm. 5) A monitor module. 6) The panel reverses polarity to sound them, and diodes or EOL resistors supervise the circuit in standby. 7) No; the panel stays in alarm until it's reset. 8) Shows which device and location is in alarm, away from the panel.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":3,"title":"Initiating Devices","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> pick the right detector for a space, explain how each type senses fire, place pull stations, and connect sprinkler waterflow and valve supervisory switches correctly.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"3.1","title":"Smoke detectors","html":"<p><strong>Photoelectric</strong> detectors shine a light inside a chamber; smoke scatters the light onto a sensor. They respond best to <strong>smoldering fires</strong> with large, visible smoke particles (a cigarette in a couch, an overheated wire). Most new commercial spot detectors are photoelectric.</p>\n<p><strong>Ionization</strong> detectors use a tiny radioactive source to make the air in a chamber conduct a small current; smoke particles disrupt it. They respond faster to <strong>fast, flaming fires</strong> with small particles. They contain a radioactive source, so follow the manufacturer's disposal instructions; don't throw them in the trash.</p>\n<p><strong>Multi-criteria</strong> detectors combine photoelectric with heat, CO, or infrared sensing to respond faster and reject nuisance sources such as steam and cooking.</p>\n<p><strong>Where smoke detectors don't belong</strong> (they'll false alarm or fail early):</p>\n<ul><li>Kitchens, bathrooms with showers, garages, and other dusty, steamy, or exhaust-filled spaces</li><li>Places outside the detector's listed temperature and humidity range, commonly about <strong>32 °F to 100 °F</strong> and up to <strong>93% relative humidity</strong> <span class=\"src\">(Detector listing; NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Directly in the airflow from supply diffusers or near return grilles (<a href=\"#/fire/learn/7\">Module 7</a>)</li></ul>\n<p>Use a heat detector in those spaces instead, when the design allows.</p>\n<p><strong>Two-wire vs four-wire:</strong> on conventional systems, <strong>two-wire</strong> smoke detectors get power and signal on the same two IDC wires and must be <strong>listed as compatible</strong> with that panel's zone. <strong>Four-wire</strong> detectors use two wires for the alarm contact and two for resettable power, with an <strong>EOL power supervision relay</strong> at the end of the power run so the panel knows if power is lost.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"3.2","title":"Heat detectors","html":"<p>Heat detectors respond to heat, not smoke. They're slower but nearly immune to nuisance alarms, so they're used where smoke detectors would false alarm. <strong>A heat detector is property protection; it doesn't replace a smoke detector where smoke detection is required for life safety.</strong></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>How it works</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Fixed temperature</strong></td><td>Alarms when the air reaches a set temperature, commonly <strong>135 °F</strong> for ordinary spaces or <strong>194 °F</strong> for hot spaces like attics and boiler rooms <span class=\"src\">(UL 521 listings)</span></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rate-of-rise (ROR)</strong></td><td>Alarms when temperature climbs fast, commonly about <strong>15 °F per minute</strong>, even if it hasn't reached the fixed setting <span class=\"src\">(UL 521 listings)</span></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Combination</strong></td><td>Fixed temperature plus rate-of-rise in one head</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Rate-compensated</strong></td><td>Accounts for how fast the air is heating so it responds close to the actual set point</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Linear heat cable</strong></td><td>A cable that detects heat anywhere along its length (tunnels, conveyors, cable trays)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><strong>Pick the rating for the space:</strong> the detector's temperature rating should be at least <strong>20 °F above the highest ceiling temperature</strong> the space normally reaches. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li><strong>Restorable vs non-restorable:</strong> some fixed-temperature heads use a fusible element that melts and must be replaced after an alarm. Check before you test with a heat gun; you can't test a non-restorable head that way.</li><li><strong>Rate-of-rise</strong> heads can false alarm in spaces that heat up fast on purpose (near ovens, loading dock doors in winter). Use fixed temperature there.</li><li>Heat detectors have a <strong>listed spacing</strong> (often 50 ft or less) that's reduced for higher ceilings. Use the spacing on the detector's listing, not the smoke detector spacing.</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"3.3","title":"Manual pull stations","html":"<p>A <strong>manual fire alarm box (pull station)</strong> lets a person start the alarm.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Single-action:</strong> pull the handle. <strong>Dual-action:</strong> push or lift a flap, then pull. Dual-action, or a protective cover, helps where malicious pulls are a problem. Covers that sound a local horn when lifted are common in schools.</li><li><strong>Location:</strong> within <strong>5 feet of each exit doorway</strong> on each floor. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li><strong>Height:</strong> the operable part between <strong>42 and 48 inches</strong> above the floor. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li><strong>Travel distance:</strong> no more than <strong>200 feet</strong> of travel to the nearest pull station on the same floor. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li><strong>Color:</strong> red, unless the AHJ approves otherwise.</li><li><strong>Reset:</strong> most pull stations stay down until reset with a key or tool, so you can tell which one was pulled.</li></ul>\n<p>Some building codes don't require pull stations throughout a fully sprinklered building (often only one, at a location the AHJ approves). Follow the approved drawings. <span class=\"src\">(IBC/IFC 907)</span></p>","mins":2},{"id":"3.4","title":"Sprinkler waterflow and valve supervision","html":"<p>Most fire alarm systems monitor the building's sprinkler system.</p>\n<h4>Waterflow switches (alarm)</h4>\n<p>A <strong>vane-type</strong> switch sits in the sprinkler pipe; water flowing past pushes the vane and closes the contact. A <strong>pressure switch</strong> is used on dry-pipe and some other systems.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Retard (delay):</strong> waterflow switches have a built-in adjustable delay so pressure surges don't cause false alarms. The signal must still come in within <strong>90 seconds</strong> of flow equal to one sprinkler head. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17; NFPA 13)</span></li><li>Waterflow is an <strong>alarm</strong> signal. It means water is moving, which usually means a sprinkler head opened.</li><li>Test it by flowing water through the <strong>inspector's test valve</strong>, not by tripping the switch by hand, so you test the whole path. Coordinate with the sprinkler contractor or building engineer.</li></ul>\n<h4>Valve supervisory switches (tamper switches)</h4>\n<p>A <strong>tamper switch</strong> mounts on a sprinkler control valve and reports when the valve is moved toward closed.</p>\n<ul><li>Must signal within <strong>two revolutions</strong> of the handwheel, or when the valve has moved <strong>one-fifth</strong> of its travel from fully open. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Must restore only when the valve is fully open again. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>It's a <strong>supervisory</strong> signal, not an alarm: a closed valve means the sprinklers may not work.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Never wire a tamper switch on the same circuit or zone as a waterflow switch</strong>, or a closed valve could look like a fire (or a fire could be missed). Supervisory and alarm signals must be distinct at the panel. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 23)</span></p>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: how you set the retard on a waterflow switch, and the setting you use most.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"3.5","title":"Other initiating devices","html":"<ul><li><strong>Duct smoke detectors:</strong> sample air in an HVAC duct to shut down the air handler and stop it spreading smoke. Covered in <a href=\"#/fire/learn/7\">Module 7</a>.</li><li><strong>Projected beam detectors:</strong> a transmitter and receiver (or reflector) across a large open space like a warehouse or atrium. Alignment matters; building movement can cause troubles.</li><li><strong>Air sampling (aspirating) detectors:</strong> pipes pull air back to a very sensitive detector. Used in data centers, clean rooms, and places where you can't reach the ceiling to service spot detectors.</li><li><strong>Flame detectors:</strong> see the infrared or ultraviolet light of a flame. Used for fuel and chemical hazards.</li><li><strong>Carbon monoxide (CO) detectors:</strong> detect CO from fuel-burning appliances. CO is a separate signal from fire, with its own notification pattern (<a href=\"#/fire/learn/4\">Module 4</a>).</li><li><strong>Monitor modules:</strong> let an addressable system watch any dry contact (waterflow, tamper, fire pump, suppression panel).</li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Which type of smoke detector responds best to a smoldering fire?","Why shouldn't you put a smoke detector in a kitchen?","What does a four-wire smoke detector circuit need at the end of the power run?","What does rate-of-rise detect?","Why do waterflow switches have a retard?","Is a closed sprinkler valve an alarm, a supervisory, or a trouble signal?","Why can't a tamper switch share a zone with a waterflow switch?","How should you test a waterflow switch?"],"answers":["Photoelectric","Cooking smoke and steam cause nuisance alarms; use a heat detector if the design allows","An EOL power supervision relay","A fast rise in temperature, even below the fixed setting","So pressure surges in the pipe don't cause false alarms","Supervisory","Alarm and supervisory signals must be distinct; a closed valve could otherwise look like a fire","Flow water through the inspector's test valve, coordinated with the sprinkler contractor or building"],"keyHtml":"1) Photoelectric. 2) Cooking smoke and steam cause nuisance alarms; use a heat detector if the design allows. 3) An EOL power supervision relay. 4) A fast rise in temperature, even below the fixed setting. 5) So pressure surges in the pipe don't cause false alarms. 6) Supervisory. 7) Alarm and supervisory signals must be distinct; a closed valve could otherwise look like a fire. 8) Flow water through the inspector's test valve, coordinated with the sprinkler contractor or building.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":4,"title":"Notification Appliances","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> explain the evacuation signal, check that horns are loud enough, choose and place strobes by candela, keep strobes synchronized, and recognize voice evacuation systems.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"4.1","title":"Audible signals and the temporal-3 pattern","html":"<p>Every fire evacuation signal in the US uses the same rhythm so people recognize it anywhere: the <strong>temporal-3</strong> pattern.</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><pre><code>ON ½s · off ½s · ON ½s · off ½s · ON ½s · off 1½s · repeat</code></pre></div>\n<p>Three half-second pulses, then a pause, repeating every 4 seconds. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18; ANSI/ASA S3.41)</span></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Horns</strong> produce the pattern from the NAC (the panel or a sync module sets the rhythm).</li><li><strong>Chimes and bells</strong> are used in some occupancies; they still follow the evacuation pattern.</li><li><strong>Carbon monoxide</strong> uses a different pattern, <strong>temporal-4</strong> (four short beeps, then a pause), so people can tell CO from fire. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18; ANSI/ASA S3.41)</span></li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"4.2","title":"How loud is loud enough","html":"<p>Audibility is measured in <strong>dBA</strong> with a sound meter, <strong>5 feet above the floor</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Public mode</strong> (everyone evacuates)</td><td>At least <strong>15 dB above the average ambient</strong> sound level, or <strong>5 dB above the maximum</strong> sound level lasting 60 seconds or more, whichever is greater <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Private mode</strong> (staff only, such as a hospital)</td><td>At least <strong>10 dB above average ambient</strong>, or 5 dB above the maximum lasting 60 seconds <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Maximum</strong></td><td>No more than <strong>110 dBA</strong> at the minimum hearing distance <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Sleeping areas</strong> (hotels, dorms, apartments) need more:</p>\n<ul><li>At least 15 dB above average ambient, 5 dB above the maximum lasting 60 seconds, or <strong>75 dBA at the pillow</strong>, whichever is greater. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></li><li>A <strong>low-frequency (520 Hz) tone</strong>, because it wakes sleeping people, including those with some hearing loss, far better than a high-pitched horn. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></li></ul>\n<p>Doors, carpets, and walls cut sound a lot. A horn in the corridor rarely gets 75 dBA to a pillow behind a closed door, so sleeping rooms usually get their own appliance.</p>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the most common reason a building fails audibility at the acceptance test.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"4.3","title":"Strobes and candela","html":"<p>Strobes (visible notification) alert people who are deaf or hard of hearing, and anyone in a noisy space. Brightness is rated in <strong>candela (cd)</strong>. Many strobes are <strong>multi-candela</strong>: you set 15, 30, 75, 110, and so on with a switch, and the current draw goes up with the setting.</p>\n<p><strong>Wall mounting:</strong> the <strong>entire lens</strong> between <strong>80 and 96 inches</strong> above the floor. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></p>\n<p><strong>Flash rate:</strong> between <strong>1 and 2 flashes per second</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></p>\n<p><strong>Room spacing (one wall-mounted strobe per room, centered on a wall):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Room size</th><th>Minimum candela</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>20 × 20 ft</td><td>15 cd</td></tr><tr><td>28 × 28 ft</td><td>30 cd</td></tr><tr><td>40 × 40 ft</td><td>60 cd</td></tr><tr><td>45 × 45 ft</td><td>75 cd</td></tr><tr><td>54 × 54 ft</td><td>95 cd</td></tr><tr><td>55 × 55 ft</td><td>115 cd</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></p>\n<p>Ceiling-mounted strobes have their own table that depends on ceiling height. Two or more strobes can cover a larger room. <strong>Use the values on the approved drawings.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Corridors</strong> (20 ft wide or less): strobes within <strong>15 ft of each end</strong> and no more than <strong>100 ft apart</strong>, with a minimum of 15 cd. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></p>\n<p><strong>Sleeping rooms:</strong> <strong>177 cd</strong> if the strobe is within 24 inches of the ceiling, <strong>110 cd</strong> if it's 24 inches or more below the ceiling. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></p>","mins":2},{"id":"4.4","title":"Synchronization","html":"<p>Flashing strobes out of step can trigger seizures in people with photosensitive epilepsy. When <strong>more than two strobes</strong> can be seen from any one spot, they must flash <strong>in sync</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></p>\n<ul><li>Sync comes from the panel's NAC (built-in sync protocol) or from a <strong>sync module</strong>.</li><li>Strobes and horns from <strong>different manufacturers</strong> usually don't sync with each other. Match the sync protocol to the appliances.</li><li><strong>NAC power extenders</strong> must use the same sync protocol and be set to follow the panel, or the strobes on the extender won't match the ones on the panel.</li><li>Horn/strobes on a two-wire sync circuit can <strong>silence the horns while strobes keep flashing</strong> (common after the alarm is acknowledged, if the AHJ allows).</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"4.5","title":"Voice evacuation","html":"<p><strong>Emergency voice/alarm communication systems (EVACS)</strong> use speakers instead of horns. They play the temporal-3 tone, then a recorded message (&quot;A fire emergency has been reported in the building. Please leave by the nearest exit...&quot;). Firefighters can also make live announcements.</p>\n<ul><li>Required in many high-rises, assembly occupancies, and some schools, per the building code.</li><li>Speakers run on <strong>25 V or 70.7 V</strong> audio circuits from an amplifier, with taps (such as ¼, ½, 1, 2 W) that set loudness. <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer spec sheets)</span></li><li><strong>Intelligibility</strong> matters, not just loudness: people must understand the words. Echoes and hard surfaces make that hard, so speaker layout is engineered.</li><li>Voice systems need more battery: <strong>15 minutes</strong> of alarm at full load instead of 5. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></li><li>Some voice systems are <strong>mass notification systems (MNS)</strong> that can also announce weather, lockdown, or other emergencies.</li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Describe the temporal-3 pattern.","What pattern does carbon monoxide use?","In public mode, how far above average ambient sound must the signal be?","What tone is required in sleeping areas, and why?","What's the mounting height for a wall strobe?","When must strobes be synchronized?","Why might strobes on an NAC extender not sync with the panel's strobes?","How much alarm time does a voice evacuation system need on battery?"],"answers":["Three half-second pulses with half-second gaps, then a 1.5-second pause, repeating","Temporal-4","15 dB (or 5 dB above the 60-second maximum, whichever is greater)","A 520 Hz low-frequency tone; it wakes sleeping people better, including those with some hearing loss","The entire lens between 80 and 96 inches above the floor","When more than two strobes can be seen from one spot","The extender isn't set to the same sync protocol or isn't set to follow the panel","15 minutes at full load"],"keyHtml":"1) Three half-second pulses with half-second gaps, then a 1.5-second pause, repeating. 2) Temporal-4. 3) 15 dB (or 5 dB above the 60-second maximum, whichever is greater). 4) A 520 Hz low-frequency tone; it wakes sleeping people better, including those with some hearing loss. 5) The entire lens between 80 and 96 inches above the floor. 6) When more than two strobes can be seen from one spot. 7) The extender isn't set to the same sync protocol or isn't set to follow the panel. 8) 15 minutes at full load.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":5,"title":"Circuits and Wiring","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> wire IDCs, SLCs, and NACs in Class B and Class A, explain why T-taps are allowed on some circuits and not others, read a fire circuit with a meter, and find a ground fault.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"5.1","title":"The three circuit types","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Circuit</th><th>Carries</th><th>Typical wiring</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>IDC</strong> (initiating device circuit)</td><td>Conventional detectors, pull stations, waterflow and tamper switches</td><td>2 wires, EOL resistor at the last device</td></tr><tr><td><strong>SLC</strong> (signaling line circuit)</td><td>Addressable devices and modules, panel-to-panel networks</td><td>2 wires (often twisted, sometimes shielded)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>NAC</strong> (notification appliance circuit)</td><td>Horns, strobes, speakers</td><td>2 wires, EOL resistor at the last appliance, usually 24 VDC</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The wire size, length limit, and whether shielding is needed come from the <strong>panel's installation manual</strong>. SLC limits are often given as a maximum loop resistance and capacitance, not just a length.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"5.2","title":"Class B and Class A","html":"<p>NFPA 72 defines circuits by <strong>how they behave when something goes wrong</strong>, using classes.</p>\n<h4>Class B</h4>\n<p>A single path out to the devices, ending at an EOL device.</p>\n<ul><li>An <strong>open</strong> anywhere gives a <strong>trouble</strong>, and every device <strong>past the break stops working</strong>.</li><li>A <strong>ground fault</strong> gives a trouble.</li><li>Most common: it's cheaper and simpler.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><pre><code>PANEL + ──[D1]──[D2]──[D3]──┐\n                          [EOL]\nPANEL − ────────────────────┘</code></pre></div>\n<h4>Class A</h4>\n<p>The circuit goes out to every device and <strong>returns to the panel</strong> on a separate pair. There's no EOL; the panel supervises the loop itself.</p>\n<ul><li>A single <strong>open</strong> gives a <strong>trouble</strong>, but <strong>every device still works</strong>, because the panel feeds the circuit from both ends.</li><li>A <strong>ground fault</strong> gives a trouble.</li><li>Required by the design in some buildings, such as high-rises or where the engineer specifies it.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><pre><code>PANEL OUT ══[D1]══[D2]══[D3]══╗\n                              ║\nPANEL RET ════════════════════╝\n(each line is a pair: + and −, no EOL)</code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>The outgoing and return conductors don't run in the same cable or raceway</strong>, so one damaged cable can't take out both. The exceptions are limited, such as short drops to a device or into the panel; the drawings and the code edition in force say how far. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 12)</span></p>\n<h4>Class X</h4>\n<p>Like Class A, but it also keeps working through a <strong>short</strong> (using <strong>isolation modules</strong> between groups of devices). Common on addressable SLCs in larger buildings.</p>\n<h4>Class N</h4>\n<p>Ethernet-based pathways for newer networked and voice systems. You'll see it on newer designs.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"5.3","title":"T-taps and why they're restricted","html":"<p>A <strong>T-tap</strong> is a branch off the middle of a circuit.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Conventional IDCs and NACs: no T-taps.</strong> The EOL at the end only supervises wire that runs <em>through</em> it. A branch is unsupervised: if it breaks, the panel never knows, and those devices are silently dead. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 12; manufacturer manuals)</span></li><li><strong>Class A circuits: no T-taps.</strong> A branch breaks the loop design. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 12; manufacturer manuals)</span></li><li><strong>Class B addressable SLCs: T-taps usually allowed</strong> if the panel manufacturer permits them, because the panel polls every device and notices one that stops answering. Check the manual for limits on total wire length with taps. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 12; manufacturer manuals)</span></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Wire through every device.</strong> On conventional circuits, the wire must land on the device terminals in and out (or on separate terminals, as the device instructions show), not be twisted together with a pigtail to the device. That way, removing a device opens the circuit and the panel reports a trouble.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"5.4","title":"Reading a fire circuit with a meter","html":"<p><strong>Disconnect the circuit from the panel first</strong> (with the panel and account on test). Don't meter a circuit while it's connected; you'll read the panel, not the wire.</p>\n<p><strong>Conventional IDC with EOL:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>You read</th><th>Means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>About the EOL value</td><td>Wiring good (with all devices normal)</td></tr><tr><td>Open (OL)</td><td>Break in the wire, loose terminal, or missing device or EOL</td></tr><tr><td>Near 0 Ω</td><td>Short, or a device in alarm</td></tr><tr><td>Any reading from either wire to ground</td><td>Ground fault</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Two-wire smoke detectors have electronics across the circuit. They're polarized, so you may read different values depending on which way your leads are. Read in both directions, and expect the EOL value in one of them.</p>\n<p><strong>NAC with EOL:</strong> appliances have built-in blocking diodes, so in one direction you read about the EOL value and in the other direction you read lower (the appliances). An open in one direction means a break; a near-zero reading <strong>in both</strong> directions means a short.</p>\n<p><strong>Never use a megohmmeter (insulation tester) with devices or the panel connected.</strong> The test voltage will destroy devices. Disconnect every device first, or don't megger at all. <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer manuals; NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></p>","mins":2},{"id":"5.5","title":"Ground faults","html":"<p>A <strong>ground fault</strong> is any connection between a fire alarm conductor and earth ground: a staple through the cable, a wire touching a metal box, a damp splice. Fire panels check for ground faults on all circuits and report a <strong>trouble</strong>.</p>\n<p>A single ground fault usually doesn't stop the system from working, but a second one on another wire can create a short or an unwanted path, so <strong>every ground fault must be fixed</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Finding a ground fault:</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Note which circuit the panel names (many panels show the circuit or even polarity).</li><li>If it doesn't, disconnect circuits one at a time until the trouble clears. Wait for the panel to re-check between steps; it can take a minute.</li><li>On the faulted circuit, meter from each wire to earth ground with the circuit off the panel.</li><li>Split the circuit at an accessible device and test each half to narrow it down.</li><li>Check devices in damp spaces, exterior devices, and anything mounted in metal back boxes first.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: your fastest way to track a ground fault on a big addressable loop.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"5.6","title":"NAC power, voltage drop, and extenders","html":"<p>NACs carry the heaviest current on the system. Strobes draw the most, especially at high candela settings.</p>\n<ul><li>Each NAC has a <strong>maximum current rating</strong> (often 1.5 to 3 A per circuit). Add up every appliance on the circuit at its listed current. <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer spec sheets)</span></li><li><strong>Voltage drop</strong> matters more on NACs than anywhere else. Appliances listed for &quot;regulated 24 VDC&quot; typically work down to about <strong>16 VDC</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(UL 1971 / UL 464)</span></li><li>Calculate drop from the <strong>battery voltage at the end of standby</strong>, not a fresh 24 or 27 V. A common starting value is <strong>20.4 VDC</strong> (85% of 24 V). <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer worksheets)</span></li><li>Use the appliance's current at its <strong>minimum operating voltage</strong> if the manufacturer lists it. Strobes draw more current as voltage drops.</li><li>When a NAC is too long or too loaded: use heavier wire, split the circuit, or add a <strong>NAC power extender</strong> near the appliances. Extenders have their own batteries and must be supervised by the panel.</li></ul>\n<p>Use the <strong><a href=\"#/fire/calculators/nac\">NAC Voltage Drop calculator</a></strong>.</p>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["Which circuit type carries addressable devices?","On a Class B IDC, what happens to devices past an open?","On a Class A circuit with one open, do the devices still work?","Why are T-taps not allowed on conventional IDCs?","What should you disconnect before using a megohmmeter?","Is a single ground fault OK to leave if the system still works?","Why should NAC voltage drop be calculated from 20.4 V instead of 24 V?","Name two ways to fix a NAC that has too much voltage drop."],"answers":["The SLC (signaling line circuit)","They stop working, and the panel shows a trouble","Yes; the panel feeds the loop from both ends","The EOL can't supervise a branch, so a break on it would go unnoticed","Every device and the panel","No; every ground fault must be fixed","It's a common value for the battery at the end of standby, the worst case when the alarm sounds","Heavier wire, splitting the circuit, or a NAC power extender near the appliances"],"keyHtml":"1) The SLC (signaling line circuit). 2) They stop working, and the panel shows a trouble. 3) Yes; the panel feeds the loop from both ends. 4) The EOL can't supervise a branch, so a break on it would go unnoticed. 5) Every device and the panel. 6) No; every ground fault must be fixed. 7) It's a common value for the battery at the end of standby, the worst case when the alarm sounds. 8) Heavier wire, splitting the circuit, or a NAC power extender near the appliances.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":6,"title":"Power Supplies and Batteries","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> set up primary power the way the code expects, size standby batteries, recognize power troubles, and maintain batteries so they're ready when the power fails.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"6.1","title":"Primary power","html":"<p>The fire alarm panel's main power comes from a <strong>dedicated branch circuit</strong>: a breaker that feeds the fire alarm and nothing else (other fire alarm equipment, like NAC extenders, can share it if the design allows). <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; NEC 760)</span></p>\n<p>The breaker must be:</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Marked in red</strong> and identified as <strong>&quot;FIRE ALARM CIRCUIT&quot;</strong> <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; NEC 760)</span></li><li><strong>Locked</strong> or otherwise protected so it can't be turned off by accident, and accessible only to authorized people <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; NEC 760)</span></li><li><strong>Recorded at the panel</strong>: the location of the panel board and breaker number is written at the fire alarm control unit <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; NEC 760)</span></li></ul>\n<p><strong>No plug-in transformers and no GFCI or AFCI breakers</strong> on fire alarm primary power unless the manufacturer and AHJ allow it. A nuisance trip would take the system to battery without anyone noticing until the battery dies. <span class=\"src\">(NEC 760)</span></p>\n<p>An electrician usually installs the branch circuit. Check it before you terminate: correct breaker, red marking, lock, and a panel label.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"6.2","title":"Secondary power (batteries)","html":"<p>When AC fails, batteries take over automatically with no loss of signals. The batteries must run the system for:</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Requirement</th><th>Typical value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Standby</strong></td><td><strong>24 hours</strong> of normal (non-alarm) operation <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Then alarm</strong> (horns and strobes)</td><td><strong>5 minutes</strong> with every notification appliance operating <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Then alarm</strong> (voice evacuation)</td><td><strong>15 minutes</strong> at maximum connected load <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Battery calculations add a <strong>20% safety margin</strong> on top of the calculated amp-hours. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></li><li>A generator can supplement batteries, but the batteries still need to carry the system for at least the transfer time, and the exact standby required with a generator depends on the code edition and AHJ. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></li><li>Most panels use <strong>two 12 V sealed lead-acid batteries in series</strong> for 24 V. Both must be the same size, age, and brand.</li><li>The panel's charger can only charge a battery up to a certain size. Check the panel's <strong>maximum battery</strong> before specifying a big one; use an external charger if the manual allows.</li></ul>\n<p>Use the <strong><a href=\"#/fire/calculators/firebatt\">Fire Battery calculator</a></strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>The formula:</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Required Ah = [(standby current in A × 24 h) + (alarm current in A × alarm hours)] × 1.2</strong></p>\n<p>Alarm hours: 5 minutes = 0.083 h; 15 minutes = 0.25 h.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"6.3","title":"Power troubles and what they mean","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Panel shows</th><th>Usually means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>AC loss / AC trouble</strong></td><td>Breaker off or tripped, wiring fault, blown panel fuse</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Low battery</strong></td><td>Batteries discharged, failing, or charger problem</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Battery trouble / missing</strong></td><td>Battery disconnected, loose lead, blown battery fuse</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Ground fault</strong></td><td>A field wire or the power wiring touching ground</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Charger trouble</strong></td><td>Panel's charger circuit has failed</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>AC loss reporting delay:</strong> the panel reports AC loss locally right away, but the transmission to the monitoring center is delayed (commonly <strong>1 to 3 hours</strong>) so short outages don't flood the monitoring center. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></p>","mins":2},{"id":"6.4","title":"Battery care and replacement","html":"<ul><li><strong>Date every battery</strong> with the month and year of manufacture or installation when you install it. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10 and 14)</span></li><li><strong>Sealed lead-acid batteries</strong> are commonly replaced every <strong>5 years</strong> (or sooner per the manufacturer or if they fail a test). <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10 and 14)</span></li><li>The charger must be able to recharge a fully discharged battery within <strong>48 hours</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></li><li>At inspection, batteries are checked visually, voltage-checked under load, and load or capacity tested on the schedule in NFPA 72. A battery that reads full voltage with no load can still collapse under load. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li><strong>Replace both batteries together.</strong> A new battery in series with an old one will be dragged down.</li><li>Keep batteries off concrete floors in cold areas and away from heat; temperature shortens battery life.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Safe battery handling:</strong> disconnect the battery leads before AC when powering down, and reconnect AC before batteries when powering up (or follow the panel manual's order). Don't let tools bridge the terminals. Recycle old batteries; don't throw them out.</p>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: what you write on a battery when you install it, and where.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"6.5","title":"NAC power extenders and remote power supplies","html":"<p>When the panel can't power all the NACs or devices, a <strong>NAC power extender (booster)</strong> or remote power supply is added.</p>\n<ul><li>It has its <strong>own primary power</strong> (dedicated branch circuit, marked and locked like the panel's) and its <strong>own batteries</strong>. Include it in the battery calculations.</li><li>It's triggered by the panel's NAC or a control module, and it <strong>reports its troubles</strong> (AC loss, low battery, ground fault, NAC trouble) back to the panel. An unsupervised power supply isn't acceptable.</li><li>Set its sync to match the panel (<a href=\"#/fire/learn/4\">Module 4</a>).</li><li>Label it with its circuit and what it powers.</li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["What does &quot;dedicated branch circuit&quot; mean?","How must the fire alarm breaker be marked?","How long must batteries carry the system in standby before an alarm?","How long must the alarm run on battery for a horn/strobe system? For a voice system?","What safety margin goes on top of the battery calculation?","Why replace both batteries at the same time?","Why is AC loss reporting to the monitoring center delayed?","What does a NAC power extender need besides AC power?"],"answers":["A breaker that feeds only the fire alarm system","In red, identified as &quot;FIRE ALARM CIRCUIT&quot;, locked, with its location recorded at the panel","24 hours","5 minutes; 15 minutes","20%","A new battery in series with an old one gets dragged down by the old one","So short power blips don't flood the monitoring center with signals","Its own batteries, supervision back to the panel, and matching sync"],"keyHtml":"1) A breaker that feeds only the fire alarm system. 2) In red, identified as &quot;FIRE ALARM CIRCUIT&quot;, locked, with its location recorded at the panel. 3) 24 hours. 4) 5 minutes; 15 minutes. 5) 20%. 6) A new battery in series with an old one gets dragged down by the old one. 7) So short power blips don't flood the monitoring center with signals. 8) Its own batteries, supervision back to the panel, and matching sync.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":7,"title":"Installation and Device Placement","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> read a fire alarm drawing, place smoke and heat detectors correctly, install duct detectors, wire elevator and HVAC control functions, and protect devices during construction.</p>","lessons":[{"id":"7.1","title":"Working from the drawings","html":"<p>Fire alarm work starts with <strong>approved drawings</strong>: the plans the AHJ reviewed. They show every device, its type and setting (candela, temperature, address), the circuits, the panel location, and a <strong>sequence of operations</strong> (what happens when each device activates).</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Install what's on the drawings.</strong> If a device can't go where it's shown (a duct, a beam, a light fixture in the way), get the change approved by the designer. Small moves can change coverage and break the approval.</li><li><strong>Keep a set of red-line drawings</strong> on site. Mark every change, the real wire routes, and device addresses. These become the <strong>as-built</strong> (record) drawings.</li><li>Check the <strong>edition of NFPA 72</strong> and local amendments listed on the drawings.</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"7.2","title":"Smoke detector placement","html":"<p>These are the basic rules for <strong>spot-type smoke detectors on smooth, flat ceilings</strong>. Beams, joists, sloped ceilings, and high ceilings change the rules. Follow the drawings.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Spacing:</strong> a nominal <strong>30 ft</strong> spacing between detectors, and no more than <strong>15 ft</strong> from a wall. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li><strong>Every point on the ceiling</strong> must be within <strong>21 ft</strong> of a detector (0.7 × the 30 ft spacing). This matters in odd-shaped rooms and long narrow corridors. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li><strong>Ceiling or wall:</strong> on the ceiling, or on a sidewall with the <strong>top of the detector within 12 inches</strong> of the ceiling. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17 and 29)</span></li><li><strong>Air movement:</strong> keep detectors at least <strong>3 ft from supply air diffusers</strong> and return air openings. Air blowing across a detector keeps smoke out of it, and dust from a supply vent makes it dirty fast. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17 and 29)</span></li><li><strong>Not in a dead-air spot:</strong> avoid the very peak of a pitched ceiling and the corner where wall meets ceiling, per the device instructions.</li><li><strong>Access:</strong> leave room to service and test it. A detector in an unreachable spot won't get tested.</li></ul>\n<p>Residential smoke <strong>alarms</strong> (stand-alone, household) follow the household chapter of NFPA 72, which keeps a <strong>4-inch</strong> rule: on the ceiling at least 4 inches from the wall, or on the wall 4 to 12 inches below the ceiling. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17 and 29)</span></p>","mins":2},{"id":"7.3","title":"Heat detector placement","html":"<ul><li>Use the <strong>listed spacing</strong> for that detector (on the label or instructions), for example 50 × 50 ft. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li><strong>Reduce spacing for high ceilings:</strong> the listed spacing assumes a ceiling about 10 ft high; above that, a reduction factor applies. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Every point on the ceiling within <strong>0.7 × the listed spacing</strong> of a detector, as for smoke. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Choose the temperature rating for the hottest normal ceiling temperature (<a href=\"#/fire/learn/3\">Module 3</a>).</li></ul>","mins":2},{"id":"7.4","title":"Duct smoke detectors","html":"<p>Duct detectors stop an air handler from <strong>spreading smoke</strong> through the building. They don't replace area smoke detectors.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Where required:</strong> NFPA 90A and the mechanical code call for them on air handlers over a certain size. A commonly cited trigger is supply systems over <strong>2,000 CFM</strong>, detecting downstream of the filters. Larger systems (commonly over <strong>15,000 CFM</strong>) may also need return-side detection. Follow the mechanical drawings. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 90A; IMC)</span></li><li><strong>Signal type:</strong> a duct detector is usually set up as a <strong>supervisory</strong> signal that shuts down the fan, rather than a building-wide alarm, unless the AHJ or design calls for an alarm. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17 and 21; NFPA 90A)</span></li><li><strong>Installation:</strong> sampling tube across the duct with holes facing the airflow, in a straight section where air isn't turbulent (often 6 to 10 duct widths from bends or dampers, per the instructions). Check the duct's air velocity is within the detector's listed range.</li><li><strong>Remote test station or indicator:</strong> required where the detector isn't easy to see and reach, so it can be tested and its alarm LED seen from the floor. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li><strong>Test</strong> it with the manufacturer's method (smoke through the test port, or a magnet test for function only, plus an airflow check across the sampling tubes).</li></ul>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the mistake you see most often on duct detector installs.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"7.5","title":"Control functions: elevators, HVAC, doors","html":"<p>The fire alarm often controls other systems. These are installed per the <strong>sequence of operations</strong> and tested with the other trades.</p>\n<p><strong>Elevator recall (Phase I):</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Smoke detectors in each elevator lobby, the machine room, and the hoistway (when required) recall elevators to the main floor, or to an alternate floor if the main lobby detector is the one in alarm. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 21; ASME A17.1)</span></li><li><strong>Shunt trip:</strong> where sprinklers are in an elevator machine room or hoistway, <strong>heat detectors</strong> within <strong>2 ft of each sprinkler head</strong>, with a lower temperature rating and faster response than the sprinkler, disconnect elevator power <strong>before</strong> water flows. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></li><li>The fire alarm provides <strong>dry contacts</strong> to the elevator controller; the elevator contractor wires their side. Test together.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Control relays:</strong> relays or control modules for emergency control functions (HVAC shutdown, elevator recall, door release) go within <strong>3 ft</strong> of the controlled device, with the wiring to the relay supervised. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></p>\n<p><strong>HVAC shutdown:</strong> the fire alarm relay stops the air handler (and closes smoke dampers where the design calls for it) per the sequence of operations.</p>\n<p><strong>Door holders and locks:</strong> magnetic door holders release fire doors on alarm. Electrically locked doors on egress routes unlock on alarm per the building code. Coordinate with the door hardware installer.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"7.6","title":"Panels, annunciators, and construction protection","html":"<ul><li><strong>Panel location:</strong> where the drawings show, usually near the main entrance or a fire command center, with working space in front.</li><li><strong>Annunciator:</strong> at the entrance firefighters will use, as approved by the AHJ.</li><li><strong>Label everything:</strong> circuits in the panel, device addresses on the devices (where allowed), and a zone or point list in the panel.</li><li><strong>Protect detectors during construction:</strong> smoke detectors installed before construction cleaning is finished get filled with drywall dust. Don't install heads until the area is clean, or cover them with the manufacturer's dust covers and <strong>remove every cover</strong> before the system goes into service. A covered detector can't see smoke. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["What do you do if a device can't be installed where the drawing shows?","What's the nominal spacing for spot smoke detectors on a smooth ceiling?","How far should a smoke detector be from a supply air diffuser?","Does a duct detector replace an area smoke detector?","What signal does a duct detector usually send?","Why do shunt trip heat detectors need a lower rating than the sprinkler heads?","What's the risk of installing smoke detectors before construction cleaning?","What are red-line drawings for?"],"answers":["Get the change approved by the designer","30 ft, with every point within 21 ft of a detector","At least 3 ft","No","Supervisory, unless the AHJ or design calls for alarm","So elevator power is cut before water flows on the elevator equipment","Dust contaminates them; they get dirty or false alarm. Use dust covers and remove them before service","To record every change and the real routing so as-built drawings can be made"],"keyHtml":"1) Get the change approved by the designer. 2) 30 ft, with every point within 21 ft of a detector. 3) At least 3 ft. 4) No. 5) Supervisory, unless the AHJ or design calls for alarm. 6) So elevator power is cut before water flows on the elevator equipment. 7) Dust contaminates them; they get dirty or false alarm. Use dust covers and remove them before service. 8) To record every change and the real routing so as-built drawings can be made.","noteHtml":""}},{"num":8,"title":"Testing, Inspection, and Maintenance","introHtml":"<p><strong>You'll be able to:</strong> run an acceptance test, follow the inspection and testing schedule, test each device type the right way, handle an impaired system, and leave the paperwork the AHJ expects.</p>\n<aside class=\"callout\"><p>NFPA 72's testing chapter has a detailed table of methods and frequencies. This module covers the common items. The edition the AHJ has adopted and the manufacturer's instructions always win.</p></aside>","lessons":[{"id":"8.1","title":"The acceptance test","html":"<p>Before a new system (or a big change) goes into service, it gets a <strong>100% acceptance test</strong>: every device and every function, often witnessed by the AHJ.</p>\n<ol><li><strong>Pre-test it yourself first.</strong> Never show an inspector a system you haven't already tested completely.</li><li>Test <strong>every initiating device</strong>: each one causes the right signal, at the right address or zone, with the right label.</li><li>Test <strong>every notification appliance</strong>: sounds and flashes, sync is correct, audibility is measured where required.</li><li>Test <strong>every control function</strong> against the sequence of operations: elevator recall, HVAC shutdown, door release, with the other trades present.</li><li>Test <strong>circuit supervision</strong>: open, ground, and (for Class A or X) shorts on each circuit produce the right trouble.</li><li>Test <strong>power</strong>: AC loss, battery operation, battery calculation checked against actual current.</li><li>Test <strong>off-site reporting</strong>: alarm, supervisory, and trouble signals reach the monitoring center with the right account and point.</li><li>Fill out the <strong>Record of Completion</strong> and give the owner the documentation (<a href=\"#/fire/learn/8/8.5\">Lesson 8.5</a>).</li></ol>","mins":2},{"id":"8.2","title":"Inspection and testing schedule","html":"<p>After acceptance, the <strong>owner</strong> is responsible for periodic inspection, testing, and maintenance. Common frequencies:</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Common frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Control panel, trouble signals, power</td><td>Visual inspection semiannually or annually, functional test <strong>annually</strong> <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Smoke, heat (restorable), duct detectors, pull stations</td><td>Functional test <strong>annually</strong> <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Notification appliances</td><td>Functional test <strong>annually</strong> <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Waterflow and valve supervisory switches</td><td><strong>Semiannually</strong> <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14; NFPA 25)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Batteries (sealed lead-acid)</td><td>Inspect and test <strong>semiannually</strong>, replace about every <strong>5 years</strong> <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Monitoring transmission</td><td>Tested <strong>annually</strong>, plus automatic test signals daily or more often <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Smoke detector sensitivity</td><td>See <a href=\"#/fire/learn/8/8.3\">Lesson 8.3</a></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Sprinkler-related devices are often also on the sprinkler inspection schedule (NFPA 25); coordinate with the sprinkler inspector so a valve closed for their test doesn't surprise you.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"8.3","title":"How to test each device","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Device</th><th>Test method</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Smoke detector</strong></td><td>Listed aerosol smoke or a smoke generator that actually puts smoke into the chamber. A magnet test only checks the electronics, not the chamber. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Smoke sensitivity</strong></td><td>Measure with the panel's sensitivity readout (addressable) or a listed sensitivity tester. Required within <strong>1 year</strong> after install, then every <strong>other year</strong>. If results stay in range, the interval can be extended up to <strong>5 years</strong>. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Heat detector (restorable)</strong></td><td>Listed heat source (heat gun or cup tester), within the manufacturer's guidelines. Don't overheat plastic housings.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Heat detector (non-restorable)</strong></td><td>Not heat tested; test the circuit with a mechanical or electrical method. Replaced or sample-tested after a set number of years per NFPA 72. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td><strong>Pull station</strong></td><td>Operate it as a person would, then reset it.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Duct detector</strong></td><td>Smoke through the test port or as the manufacturer directs, plus confirm airflow in the sampling tubes.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Waterflow switch</strong></td><td>Flow water through the inspector's test valve; time the signal.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Tamper switch</strong></td><td>Close the valve (with the sprinkler contractor or building), confirm the signal by two turns, open fully, confirm restore.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Horn/strobe</strong></td><td>Activate and confirm every appliance works and sync looks right.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Never use an open flame</strong> to test a detector. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></p>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: how you keep track of which devices you've tested on a large building.</p></aside>","mins":2},{"id":"8.4","title":"Impairments, fire watch, and nuisance alarms","html":"<p>An <strong>impairment</strong> is any time the system (or part of it) can't do its job: a circuit disabled, a panel down for repair, devices removed during a remodel.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Notify the AHJ and the owner</strong> when the system will be out of service for more than <strong>4 hours in a 24-hour period</strong>, and the monitoring center whenever signals will be affected. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; IFC 901.7)</span></li><li>The AHJ may require a <strong>fire watch</strong>: trained people walking the building looking for fire, with a way to call the fire department, until the system is restored. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; IFC 901.7)</span></li><li><strong>Tag the panel</strong> with what's impaired and who to call, and remove the tag when you restore it.</li><li><strong>Never leave a site</strong> with a circuit, device, or output disabled unless the owner and AHJ know and the impairment is documented.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Nuisance alarms</strong> (an alarm caused by something other than fire, like dust, steam, or a bad device) erode trust in the system and get people to ignore real alarms. Fix the cause: clean or replace the detector, move it per the design, or change the detector type with the designer's approval. <strong>Don't</strong> fix a nuisance alarm by disabling the device and leaving it.</p>","mins":2},{"id":"8.5","title":"Documentation","html":"<p>Every fire job produces paperwork. Leave it complete; the next tech and the inspector depend on it.</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Record of Completion:</strong> the form (from NFPA 72) describing the system, its power, circuits, devices, and monitoring, signed at acceptance.</li><li><strong>Record (as-built) drawings, sequence of operations, battery and voltage drop calculations, and manufacturer manuals:</strong> kept on site, often in a <strong>documentation cabinet</strong> at the panel. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 7 and 14)</span></li><li><strong>Inspection and test reports:</strong> a record of each device tested, the result, and any deficiencies. Retained at least until the next test plus one year. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 7 and 14)</span></li><li><strong>Site-specific software and passwords:</strong> a copy of the panel program kept on site or with the owner, so the system can be serviced if the original installer is gone. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 7 and 14)</span></li></ul>","mins":2}],"quiz":{"questions":["What's the first step before an AHJ witnesses an acceptance test?","Who is responsible for keeping the system inspected and tested after acceptance?","How often are smoke detectors functionally tested?","Does a magnet test prove a smoke detector's chamber works?","When is the first sensitivity test due on a new smoke detector?","How long can a system be out of service before you must notify the AHJ?","What's a fire watch?","Name three documents that stay on site with the system."],"answers":["Pre-test the whole system yourself","The owner","Annually","No; only listed smoke or a smoke generator tests the chamber","Within 1 year after installation","More than 4 hours in a 24-hour period","Trained people patrolling the building for fire while the system is impaired","Record of Completion, as-built drawings, sequence of operations, battery and voltage drop calculations, manuals, test reports, site-specific software"],"keyHtml":"1) Pre-test the whole system yourself. 2) The owner. 3) Annually. 4) No; only listed smoke or a smoke generator tests the chamber. 5) Within 1 year after installation. 6) More than 4 hours in a 24-hour period. 7) Trained people patrolling the building for fire while the system is impaired. 8) Record of Completion, as-built drawings, sequence of operations, battery and voltage drop calculations, manuals, test reports, site-specific software.","noteHtml":""}}]},"reference":{"introHtml":"<p>Quick cards for the Reference tab. Each <code>##</code> section is one card. Values are common NFPA 72 based figures; the approved drawings, the AHJ's adopted edition, and the installation manual always win.</p>","cards":[{"id":"signal-types-at-a-glance","title":"Signal types at a glance","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>Means</th><th>Examples</th><th>Monitoring center</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Alarm</strong></td><td>Possible fire</td><td>Smoke, heat, pull station, waterflow</td><td>Dispatches fire department</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Supervisory</strong></td><td>Fire protection off-normal</td><td>Valve tamper, low air, duct detector (usually), fire pump</td><td>Calls the building</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Trouble</strong></td><td>Fire alarm system fault</td><td>Open, ground, AC loss, low battery, missing device</td><td>Calls the service company</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Priority: alarm, then supervisory, then trouble. Alarm and supervisory latch until reset.</p>"},{"id":"before-you-test","title":"Before you test","html":"<ol><li>Call the monitoring center and put the account <strong>on test</strong>. Get the operator's name. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li>Notify the owner or building contact, plus occupants if appliances will sound. Some jurisdictions also require notifying the fire department. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li><strong>Disable releasing circuits</strong> (suppression) and any output you don't want to run: elevator recall, HVAC shutdown, door unlock. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li>Coordinate with sprinkler, elevator, and HVAC trades if their equipment is part of the test.</li><li>When finished: re-enable everything, reset, panel <strong>normal with no troubles</strong>, account <strong>off test</strong> with the operator.</li></ol>"},{"id":"circuit-classes","title":"Circuit classes","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><pre><code>Class B (one path, EOL at the end)\nPANEL ──[D1]──[D2]──[D3]──[EOL]\nOpen = trouble, devices past the break are lost\n\nClass A (out and back, no EOL)\nPANEL OUT ══[D1]══[D2]══[D3]══╗\nPANEL RET ════════════════════╝\nOpen = trouble, every device still works\n\nClass X = Class A + isolators, survives a short</code></pre></div>\n<ul><li>No T-taps on conventional IDCs, NACs, or any Class A circuit. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 12; manufacturer manuals)</span></li><li>T-taps on Class B addressable SLC only if the manufacturer allows. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 12; manufacturer manuals)</span></li><li>Class A outgoing and return never in the same cable or raceway, except limited short runs such as drops to a device or into the panel. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 12)</span></li></ul>"},{"id":"meter-readings-on-fire-circuits","title":"Meter readings on fire circuits","html":"<p>Circuit <strong>disconnected</strong> from the panel, panel and account on test.</p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Reading</th><th>Conventional IDC</th><th>NAC (appliances polarized)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>≈ EOL value</td><td>Good</td><td>Good (in the reverse direction)</td></tr><tr><td>Lower than EOL one way, EOL the other</td><td>Normal for two-wire smokes</td><td>Normal (appliances seen forward)</td></tr><tr><td>OL in both directions</td><td>Open: wire, terminal, missing device or EOL</td><td>Open</td></tr><tr><td>≈ 0 Ω both directions</td><td>Short, or a device in alarm</td><td>Short</td></tr><tr><td>Any reading to earth ground</td><td>Ground fault</td><td>Ground fault</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>No megohmmeter with devices or the panel connected.</strong> <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer manuals; NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></p>"},{"id":"smoke-detector-placement","title":"Smoke detector placement","html":"<p>Smooth flat ceiling, spot-type. Follow the drawings for beams, slopes, and high ceilings.</p>\n<ul><li>30 ft nominal spacing, no more than 15 ft from a wall <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Every ceiling point within 21 ft of a detector <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Ceiling, or sidewall with the top of detector within 12 in of the ceiling <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17 and 29)</span></li><li>Household smoke alarms: 4 in from the wall on the ceiling, or 4 to 12 in down on the wall <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17 and 29)</span></li><li>At least 3 ft from supply diffusers and return openings <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17 and 29)</span></li><li>Not in kitchens, showers, garages, dusty spaces, or outside the listed range (commonly 32 to 100 °F, up to 93% humidity) <span class=\"src\">(Detector listing; NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Dust covers during construction; remove every one before service <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li></ul>"},{"id":"heat-detector-quick-rules","title":"Heat detector quick rules","html":"<ul><li>Rating at least 20 °F above the hottest normal ceiling temperature <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Common ratings: 135 °F ordinary, 194 °F hot spaces <span class=\"src\">(UL 521 listings)</span></li><li>Rate-of-rise: commonly about 15 °F per minute <span class=\"src\">(UL 521 listings)</span></li><li>Use the <strong>listed spacing</strong>; reduce it above about 10 ft ceilings <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Every point within 0.7 × listed spacing <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Non-restorable heads can't be heat tested</li><li>Heat detectors are property protection, not a substitute for required smoke detection</li></ul>"},{"id":"pull-stations-waterflow-tamper","title":"Pull stations, waterflow, tamper","html":"<p><strong>Pull stations</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Within 5 ft of each exit doorway, each floor <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Operable part 42 to 48 in above the floor <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>No more than 200 ft travel to the nearest one <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Waterflow (alarm)</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Signal within 90 s of flow equal to one sprinkler <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17; NFPA 13)</span></li><li>Test through the inspector's test valve</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Tamper / valve supervisory (supervisory)</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Signal within 2 turns of the handwheel or 1/5 of valve travel <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Restore only when fully open <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Never on the same zone as waterflow <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 23)</span></li></ul>"},{"id":"strobe-candela-and-placement","title":"Strobe candela and placement","html":"<ul><li>Wall mount: entire lens 80 to 96 in above the floor <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></li><li>Flash rate 1 to 2 per second <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></li><li>More than 2 visible from one spot: must be synchronized <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></li></ul>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Room (one wall strobe)</th><th>Minimum cd</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>20 × 20 ft</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td>28 × 28 ft</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>40 × 40 ft</td><td>60</td></tr><tr><td>45 × 45 ft</td><td>75</td></tr><tr><td>54 × 54 ft</td><td>95</td></tr><tr><td>55 × 55 ft</td><td>115</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></p>\n<ul><li>Corridors up to 20 ft wide: 15 cd minimum, within 15 ft of each end, no more than 100 ft apart <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></li><li>Sleeping rooms: 177 cd within 24 in of the ceiling, 110 cd if 24 in or more below <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></li></ul>"},{"id":"audibility-and-tones","title":"Audibility and tones","html":"<p>Measured in dBA, 5 ft above the floor. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Minimum</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Public</td><td>15 dB over average ambient, or 5 dB over 60 s max (greater) <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Private</td><td>10 dB over average ambient, or 5 dB over 60 s max <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Sleeping</td><td>Greater of 15 dB over ambient, 5 dB over max, or 75 dBA at the pillow; 520 Hz low-frequency tone <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Maximum</td><td>110 dBA <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><pre><code>Temporal-3 (fire evacuation)\n█ _ █ _ █ ___  █ _ █ _ █ ___\n½s on/off ×3, then 1½s off, repeats every 4 s\n\nTemporal-4 (carbon monoxide)\n▌▌▌▌ _____ ▌▌▌▌ _____\n4 short pulses, then a pause</code></pre></div>\n<p><span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18; ANSI/ASA S3.41)</span></p>"},{"id":"secondary-power-batteries","title":"Secondary power (batteries)","html":"<p><strong>Required Ah = [(standby A × 24 h) + (alarm A × alarm h)] × 1.2</strong></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Standby</td><td>24 h <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Alarm, horns/strobes</td><td>5 min (0.083 h) <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Alarm, voice evacuation</td><td>15 min (0.25 h) <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Safety margin</td><td>20% <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Replace sealed lead-acid</td><td>About every 5 years, date every battery <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10 and 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Recharge time</td><td>Within 48 h <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Two 12 V batteries in series for 24 V; same size, age, and brand</li><li>Check the panel's maximum battery size and charger capability</li><li>Include NAC extenders and power supplies (each has its own batteries)</li></ul>\n<p>Use the <a href=\"#/fire/calculators/firebatt\">Fire Battery calculator</a>.</p>"},{"id":"nac-voltage-drop","title":"NAC voltage drop","html":"<ul><li>Start from <strong>20.4 V</strong> (battery at end of standby), not 24 V <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer worksheets)</span></li><li>Regulated 24 V appliances commonly work down to <strong>16 V</strong> <span class=\"src\">(UL 1971 / UL 464)</span></li><li>Use appliance current at minimum voltage when listed</li><li>NAC rating commonly 1.5 to 3 A per circuit; check the panel <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer spec sheets)</span></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Drop = 2 × one-way length (ft) × total current (A) × Ω per foot</strong></p>\n<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>AWG</th><th>Ω per foot</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>18</td><td>0.00639</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>0.00402</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>0.00253</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>0.00159</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Lumping all current at the far end is the conservative method. If it fails, the manufacturer's point-to-point calculation may still pass.</p>\n<p>Use the <a href=\"#/fire/calculators/nac\">NAC Voltage Drop calculator</a>.</p>"},{"id":"primary-power","title":"Primary power","html":"<ul><li>Dedicated branch circuit, fire alarm only <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; NEC 760)</span></li><li>Breaker marked red, &quot;FIRE ALARM CIRCUIT&quot;, locked, location recorded at the panel <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; NEC 760)</span></li><li>No GFCI or AFCI unless the manufacturer and AHJ allow <span class=\"src\">(NEC 760)</span></li><li>AC loss to monitoring delayed, commonly 1 to 3 h <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10)</span></li></ul>"},{"id":"fire-alarm-cable","title":"Fire alarm cable","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Marking</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>FPL</td><td>General</td></tr><tr><td>FPLR</td><td>Riser (and anywhere FPL is allowed)</td></tr><tr><td>FPLP</td><td>Plenum (anywhere)</td></tr><tr><td>CI</td><td>Circuit integrity, survivability</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li>Identify fire alarm circuits at terminal and junction locations (red covers and cable are common; red itself isn't required) <span class=\"src\">(NEC 760.30)</span></li><li>Keep separate from power and non-power-limited circuits</li><li>Support from structure, not ceiling grid wires</li><li>Wire size and length limits: panel manual</li></ul>"},{"id":"inspection-and-testing-frequencies","title":"Inspection and testing frequencies","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Common frequency</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Smoke, heat, duct detectors, pull stations</td><td>Annual functional test <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Horns, strobes, speakers</td><td>Annual <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Panel and power, trouble signals</td><td>Annual <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Waterflow, tamper</td><td>Semiannual <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14; NFPA 25)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Batteries</td><td>Semiannual inspection and test <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Smoke sensitivity</td><td>1 year after install, then every other year; up to 5 years if stable <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></td></tr><tr><td>Impairment over 4 h in 24 h</td><td>Notify AHJ; fire watch may be required <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; IFC 901.7)</span></td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"contact-id-fire-event-codes","title":"Contact ID fire event codes","html":"<div class=\"scroll\"><table><thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Event</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>110</td><td>Fire alarm</td></tr><tr><td>111</td><td>Smoke</td></tr><tr><td>113</td><td>Waterflow</td></tr><tr><td>114</td><td>Heat</td></tr><tr><td>115</td><td>Pull station</td></tr><tr><td>116</td><td>Duct</td></tr><tr><td>200</td><td>Fire supervisory</td></tr><tr><td>203</td><td>Gate valve (tamper)</td></tr><tr><td>301</td><td>AC loss</td></tr><tr><td>302</td><td>Low system battery</td></tr><tr><td>373</td><td>Fire trouble</td></tr><tr><td>602</td><td>Periodic test</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><span class=\"src\">(SIA DC-05)</span></p>\n<p>Qualifier 1 = new event, 3 = restore. Confirm the panel reports the specific code, not just 110, if the monitoring center needs device type.</p>"},{"id":"before-you-leave-the-job","title":"Before you leave the job","html":"<ul class=\"checklist\"><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Every disabled device, circuit, and output re-enabled</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Panel reset, normal, no troubles, no ground faults</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Account off test, confirmed with the operator</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>AC and batteries good; batteries dated</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Impairment tags removed (or impairment documented with owner and AHJ)</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Dust covers removed</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Test report filled out; deficiencies listed</span></label></li><li class=\"check\"><label><input type=\"checkbox\"><span>Documentation cabinet updated</span></label></li></ul>"},{"id":"glossary","title":"Glossary","html":"<ul><li><strong>AHJ:</strong> Authority Having Jurisdiction; usually the fire marshal.</li><li><strong>Alarm verification:</strong> the panel resets a smoke detector and rechecks before declaring an alarm.</li><li><strong>Annunciator:</strong> remote display that shows what's in alarm.</li><li><strong>Candela (cd):</strong> brightness rating of a strobe.</li><li><strong>Class A / B / X:</strong> how a circuit behaves with an open, ground, or short.</li><li><strong>DACT:</strong> digital alarm communicator transmitter; sends signals over phone lines.</li><li><strong>dBA:</strong> sound level, A-weighted, used for audibility.</li><li><strong>EVACS:</strong> emergency voice/alarm communication system.</li><li><strong>FACP / FACU:</strong> fire alarm control panel / unit.</li><li><strong>Fire watch:</strong> people patrolling for fire while a system is impaired.</li><li><strong>FPL / FPLR / FPLP:</strong> power-limited fire alarm cable ratings: general, riser, plenum.</li><li><strong>IDC:</strong> initiating device circuit (conventional).</li><li><strong>Impairment:</strong> any time all or part of the system can't do its job.</li><li><strong>Monitor module:</strong> addressable input for a dry contact.</li><li><strong>NAC:</strong> notification appliance circuit.</li><li><strong>NAC power extender:</strong> booster supply that adds NAC capacity.</li><li><strong>PLFA:</strong> power-limited fire alarm circuit.</li><li><strong>Record of Completion:</strong> NFPA 72 form documenting the installed system.</li><li><strong>Retard:</strong> adjustable delay in a waterflow switch.</li><li><strong>Sequence of operations:</strong> what happens when each device activates.</li><li><strong>Shunt trip:</strong> breaker that cuts elevator power before sprinklers discharge on it.</li><li><strong>SLC:</strong> signaling line circuit (addressable).</li><li><strong>Supervisory:</strong> signal that fire protection equipment is off-normal.</li><li><strong>Tamper switch:</strong> valve supervisory switch on a sprinkler control valve.</li><li><strong>Temporal-3:</strong> the standard fire evacuation pattern.</li><li><strong>Trouble:</strong> signal that the fire alarm system has a fault.</li><li><strong>Waterflow switch:</strong> detects water moving in a sprinkler pipe.</li></ul>"}]},"troubleshoot":{"introHtml":"<p>One guide per common fire service call, for the Troubleshoot tab. Each guide is a step-by-step flowchart: do the step, then follow the result.</p>\n<p><strong>Every guide starts the same way:</strong> put the account <strong>on test</strong> with the monitoring center, notify the building, and disable releasing circuits and any outputs you don't want to operate before you touch anything (Reference: <a href=\"#/fire/reference/before-you-test\"><em>Before you test</em></a>). <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span> <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span> When you're done, re-enable everything, reset, confirm the panel is normal, and take the account off test.</p>","guides":[{"num":1,"title":"Ground fault trouble","symptomHtml":"Panel shows a ground fault (sometimes with + or − polarity, sometimes with the circuit).","html":"<ol><li>Read the panel. Does it name a circuit or module? → Start with that circuit at step 4.</li><li>Was anything worked on recently (remodel, new devices, roof work, rain)? → Check that area first.</li><li>No circuit named: disconnect field circuits one at a time (SLCs, IDCs, NACs, communicator, annunciator, power supply outputs). Wait for the panel to re-check after each one. The ground fault clears → that circuit has it.</li><li>With the circuit off the panel, meter each conductor to earth ground. Any reading → the fault is on that conductor.</li><li>Split the circuit at an accessible midpoint and test each half. Repeat until you're down to one run or device.</li><li>Look for: a staple or screw through the cable, wire pinched against a metal box or cover, water in an exterior or basement device, a damaged conduit fitting, or a shield drain wire touching the box.</li><li><strong>No megohmmeter with devices connected.</strong> <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer manuals; NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li>Repair, reconnect, confirm the trouble clears and the circuit works.</li></ol>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: the most common place you find a ground fault in a finished building.</p></aside>"},{"num":2,"title":"Open circuit trouble (conventional IDC or NAC)","symptomHtml":"Panel shows trouble or open on a zone or NAC.","html":"<ol><li>Look for the obvious: a device removed, a detector out of its base, a ceiling tile disturbed, recent construction.</li><li>Disconnect the circuit at the panel and meter it (Reference: <a href=\"#/fire/reference/meter-readings-on-fire-circuits\"><em>Meter readings on fire circuits</em></a>).<ul><li>Reads the EOL value → wiring is good. Check the panel terminal and the circuit's programming. Try the EOL resistor directly on the panel terminals: trouble clears → field wiring issue that comes and goes; trouble stays → panel side.</li><li>Reads open → go to step 3.</li></ul></li><li>Find the EOL. Is it there and the right value? A missing or wrong EOL is common after another contractor's work.</li><li>Go device by device from the panel: check each detector is seated in its base and each terminal is tight. On two-wire smokes, a detector removed from its base opens the circuit by design.</li><li>Split the circuit at an accessible device and meter each half to find the break.</li><li>Look for a T-tap. A branch someone added will leave part of the circuit unsupervised and may hide the real break. Rewire it as a single path. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 12; manufacturer manuals)</span></li><li>Reconnect, confirm the trouble clears, and test the devices past where the break was.</li></ol>"},{"num":3,"title":"Addressable device missing, no answer, or wrong type","symptomHtml":"Panel shows a device missing, no answer, invalid reply, wrong device type, or duplicate address.","html":"<ol><li>Note the address and look it up on the point list.</li><li><strong>One device missing:</strong> go to it. Is it in its base? Is the base wiring tight? Is it damaged or painted over? Replace with the same type and set the same address.</li><li><strong>Wrong device type:</strong> the device installed doesn't match the programming (a heat detector where a smoke is programmed). Install the correct type or have the program changed by someone authorized, per the drawings.</li><li><strong>Duplicate address:</strong> two devices set to the same address. Common after a device swap. Find both and correct one.</li><li><strong>Many devices missing past one point:</strong> an open on the SLC (Class B), a short isolated by an isolator (Class X), or a damaged cable. Go to the first missing device and check the wiring in and out.</li><li><strong>Devices dropping in and out:</strong> SLC wiring too long, too much capacitance, unshielded cable near noise, a loose splice, or a ground fault. Check the panel's SLC limits and the loop's wiring.</li><li>After repair, confirm the device reports at the right address with the right label, and test it.</li></ol>"},{"num":4,"title":"Horns or strobes not working, or NAC trouble","symptomHtml":"NAC trouble on the panel, or some appliances don't sound or flash during a test.","html":"<ol><li><strong>NAC trouble with no alarm:</strong> treat as an open or short (<a href=\"#/fire/troubleshoot/2\">Guide 2</a> for opens). A <strong>short</strong> on a NAC usually shows when the panel tries to activate it; meter both directions for a short.</li><li><strong>Some appliances work, some don't:</strong><ul><li>All the dead ones past one point → open or bad splice between the last working and first dead appliance.</li><li>Random dead ones → bad appliance, appliance wired backward (polarity), or a loose terminal.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Strobes dim, slow, or not flashing at the far end:</strong> voltage drop. Measure voltage at the last appliance <strong>during alarm</strong>. Compare to the appliance's minimum (commonly 16 V). <span class=\"src\">(UL 1971 / UL 464)</span> Use the <a href=\"#/fire/calculators/nac\">NAC Voltage Drop calculator</a> to confirm and fix (heavier wire, split the circuit, or add an extender).</li><li><strong>Strobes flashing out of sync:</strong> sync protocol mismatch between panel, extender, or appliance brands; an extender not set to follow the panel; or mixed appliance models. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 18)</span></li><li><strong>NAC extender circuits dead:</strong> check the extender's AC, batteries, and trouble LEDs, and the trigger wiring from the panel.</li><li><strong>Panel shuts down the NAC:</strong> total current over the NAC rating. Add up every appliance at its setting. <span class=\"src\">(Manufacturer spec sheets)</span></li><li>Retest every appliance on the circuit after the fix.</li></ol>"},{"num":5,"title":"AC power loss or battery trouble","symptomHtml":"Panel shows AC loss, low battery, battery trouble, or charger trouble.","html":"<ol><li><strong>AC loss:</strong> check the fire alarm breaker (it should be red-marked and locked). Tripped or turned off? → Find out why before resetting. Then check the panel's AC fuse and terminal connections. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; NEC 760)</span></li><li><strong>AC good but low battery:</strong> measure battery voltage with the batteries on the charger, then under load (disconnect AC for a short test, or use a battery load tester).<ul><li>Low with AC on for more than a couple of days → charger problem or a dead battery.</li><li>Good with no load, collapses under load → battery is failing. Replace both.</li></ul></li><li>Check the date on the batteries. Older than about 5 years → replace both. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10 and 14)</span></li><li><strong>Battery trouble / missing:</strong> loose lead, blown battery fuse, corroded terminals, or a jumper missing between the two batteries.</li><li><strong>Batteries keep dying:</strong> run a battery calculation (<a href=\"#/fire/calculators/firebatt\">Fire Battery calculator</a>) against actual current. Too much load for the battery size, or a battery bigger than the charger can recharge.</li><li>Date the new batteries and confirm the trouble clears. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10 and 14)</span></li></ol>"},{"num":6,"title":"Nuisance smoke alarms","symptomHtml":"Repeated alarms from a smoke detector with no fire.","html":"<ol><li>Pull the history: same device each time? Same time of day? (Cooking, cleaning, HVAC cycling, shift change, sprinkler testing.)</li><li><strong>Addressable:</strong> check the detector's sensitivity and dirty or maintenance alert on the panel. Dirty → clean per manufacturer or replace.</li><li>Look at the environment: steam (showers, kitchens), dust (construction, warehouses), insects, exhaust, aerosol sprays, humidity, or temperature outside the listed range. <span class=\"src\">(Detector listing; NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Check the location: within 3 ft of a supply diffuser or return? Air drawing dust into it or blowing across it? <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17 and 29)</span></li><li>Construction dust? Clean or replace, and make sure dust covers are used next time. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Fix the cause: clean, replace, or relocate or change the detector type <strong>with the designer's or AHJ's approval</strong>. Don't change device types on your own.</li><li><strong>Never leave a nuisance detector disabled</strong> without notifying the owner and AHJ and documenting the impairment. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; IFC 901.7)</span></li></ol>\n<aside class=\"tip tip-open\"><div class=\"tip-h\">Field tip</div><p>Coming soon from a working tech. Topic: a nuisance alarm cause that took you a long time to find.</p></aside>"},{"num":7,"title":"Supervisory won't clear (tamper, duct, or other)","symptomHtml":"Supervisory signal on the panel that stays after a reset.","html":"<ol><li>Identify the device. Is it a valve tamper, duct detector, low air, fire pump, or other monitored input?</li><li><strong>Valve tamper:</strong> is the valve fully open? Coordinate with the building or sprinkler contractor; never open or close sprinkler valves yourself unless you're authorized. A partly closed valve won't restore. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17)</span></li><li>Valve fully open but still supervisory → adjust or replace the switch; check that the switch's trip lever sits correctly on the valve.</li><li><strong>Duct detector:</strong> check the detector's LED and the remote test station. Dirty, in alarm, or in trouble? Check airflow across the sampling tubes. Reset at the detector if it latches locally. (<a href=\"#/fire/troubleshoot/10\">Guide 10</a>)</li><li><strong>Wiring:</strong> meter the circuit. A short on a supervisory input can read as an active supervisory.</li><li>Reset the panel and confirm the supervisory clears.</li></ol>"},{"num":8,"title":"Waterflow alarm problems","symptomHtml":"Waterflow alarm with no fire, or no alarm when water flows.","html":"<ol><li><strong>False waterflow alarms:</strong> check for pressure surges (fire pump starting, city water pressure changes). Check the retard setting; it may be too short. Don't set it so long that it exceeds the 90-second requirement. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 17; NFPA 13)</span></li><li>Is water actually moving? A leak, a broken head, or an open drain will cause a real waterflow. Investigate before you call it false.</li><li>Check the switch for corrosion, water in the electrical housing, or a damaged paddle.</li><li><strong>No alarm on flow:</strong> test with the inspector's test valve with the sprinkler contractor. Time it. No signal → check the switch contact with a meter while water flows, then the wiring and module or zone.</li><li>Confirm the waterflow is not on the same zone as a tamper switch. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 23)</span></li></ol>"},{"num":9,"title":"Failure to communicate with the monitoring center","symptomHtml":"Panel shows communication trouble, or the monitoring center isn't receiving signals or periodic tests.","html":"<ol><li>Which path failed? (Phone line 1 or 2, cellular, IP.)</li><li><strong>Phone line (DACT):</strong> is there dial tone at the panel? Lines often get disconnected or moved to VoIP without anyone telling the alarm company. VoIP may not pass DACT signals reliably.</li><li><strong>Cellular:</strong> check signal strength at the communicator, the antenna connection, and that the account is active with the provider.</li><li><strong>IP:</strong> check the network cable, the switch port, and that the network still allows the communicator out.</li><li>Check the account number and receiver numbers in the panel or communicator programming.</li><li>Send a test signal and confirm with the operator that the account and event came in correctly.</li><li>Until the path is restored, the system is impaired for off-site reporting; notify the owner. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 10; IFC 901.7)</span></li></ol>"},{"num":10,"title":"Panel won't reset","symptomHtml":"The panel goes back into alarm, supervisory, or trouble right after a reset.","html":"<ol><li>Which device is still active? Find it on the panel.</li><li><strong>Pull station:</strong> still pulled down. Reset it with its key or tool.</li><li><strong>Smoke detector:</strong> smoke or dust still in the chamber. Wait, clear it, or replace it. On a <strong>four-wire</strong> zone, the panel must drop resettable power to reset the detector; check the resettable power output and the EOL relay.</li><li><strong>Duct detector:</strong> some latch at the detector and need a local reset or a reset from the remote test station.</li><li><strong>Heat detector:</strong> a non-restorable head has fused and must be replaced.</li><li><strong>Waterflow:</strong> water is still flowing.</li><li><strong>Monitor module input</strong> still closed: check the contact it watches (a suppression panel, fire pump controller, etc.).</li><li><strong>Short on an IDC</strong> looks like a device in alarm on many conventional panels. Disconnect the zone and meter it (<a href=\"#/fire/troubleshoot/2\">Guide 2</a>).</li></ol>"},{"num":11,"title":"Elevator recall or control function didn't operate","symptomHtml":"During a test, elevators didn't recall, fans didn't shut down, or doors didn't release.","html":"<ol><li>Was the output disabled for testing and not re-enabled? Check the panel's disabled list first.</li><li>Check the relay or control module: does it change state when the input is activated? Listen and meter the contact.</li><li>Check the wiring from the relay to the other trade's equipment. The relay should be within 3 ft of the controlled device with a supervised circuit to it. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 21)</span></li><li>Check the programming against the sequence of operations: is the input mapped to that output?</li><li><strong>Elevators:</strong> confirm lobby detector, machine room, and hoistway signals go to the right recall input (primary or alternate floor). Test with the elevator contractor present. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 21; ASME A17.1)</span></li><li>Retest the full sequence after the fix and document it.</li></ol>"},{"num":12,"title":"Detector or device needs replacing","symptomHtml":"A detector fails sensitivity, is damaged, or is past its service life.","html":"<ol><li>Get the exact replacement: same type, <strong>listed and compatible</strong> with the panel. A different model may need panel programming or may not be compatible at all.</li><li>Put the account on test and disable the device or zone. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li>Replace the head (or base and head), set the same address on addressable systems.</li><li>Confirm the panel sees the correct device type at the correct address, with no troubles.</li><li>Test the new device with listed smoke or the correct method. <span class=\"src\">(NFPA 72 Ch. 14)</span></li><li>Record the replacement on the test report.</li></ol>"}]},"search":[{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.1 Why fire alarm is different","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.1","text":"A burglar alarm protects property. A fire alarm protects lives . That one difference changes how the work is done: - The rules are mandatory. Most commercial fire alarm systems are required by the building or fire code, designed to NFPA 72, and inspected by the fire marshal before anyone moves in. - The design is engineered. Device locations, candela ratings, and wire sizes come from approved drawings. You install what's on the drawings; if something won't work in the field, you get it changed through the designer, not on your own. - Every change is documented. Drawings, battery and voltage drop calculations, and test records follow the system for its whole life. - The system must always be watching. Any time you take a fire system out of service, people in that building lose protection. You notify the right people before, and you put it back the way you found it after. - Liability is real. If a system fails in a fire, the install and every service ticket will be examined. Many states require a fire alarm license for the company and the technician, and many AHJs and employers require NICET certification in Fire Alarm Systems for designers and lead techs. Know what your state requires before you work on fire."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.2 The codes and who enforces them","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.2","text":"Code or standard What it covers NFPA 72 (National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code) How fire alarm systems are designed, installed, tested, and maintained NFPA 70 (National Electrical Code), Article 760 Fire alarm wiring: circuit types, cable ratings, separation IBC / IFC (International Building and Fire Codes) or NFPA 101 (Life Safety Code) When a fire alarm is required, and what kind, based on building use and size NFPA 90A HVAC systems, including where duct smoke detectors are required UL 864 The listing standard for fire alarm control units The building code says whether you need a system. NFPA 72 says how to build it. The AHJ (Authority Having Jurisdiction) is usually the fire marshal or fire prevention office. The AHJ: - Adopts a specific edition of each code, often with local amendments - Reviews and approves the drawings before installation (plan review) - Witnesses the acceptance test before the building is occupied - Can require more than the code minimum Listed equipment only. Every device, panel, and appliance must be listed for fire alarm use and listed as compatible with the panel it connects to. A smoke detector that works fine on one brand of panel may not be listed for another."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.3 Power-limited fire alarm circuits and cable","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.3","text":"Most of what you'll wire is power-limited fire alarm (PLFA) circuits: initiating device circuits, signaling line circuits, and notification appliance circuits from a listed fire alarm control unit. The panel limits the power on those circuits, so the NEC allows lighter wiring methods than for 120 V power. Fire alarm cable types (NEC Article 760) Marking Where it's used FPL General use, not in risers or plenums FPLR Riser: vertical runs between floors (also OK where FPL is allowed) FPLP Plenum: air-handling spaces above drop ceilings (OK anywhere) CI (circuit integrity) Survivability: keeps working for a rated time in a fire Rules to remember: - Fire alarm circuits are identified at terminal and junction locations so nobody mistakes them for something else and cuts power to them. Red box covers and red cable are common ways to do this, but red itself isn't required. - Keep PLFA cables separated from power, lighting, and non-power-limited circuits. Don't share a box or raceway with 120 V wiring. - Use the jacket rating for the space. Plenum spaces need FPLP (or cable in a raceway as allowed by the NEC and AHJ). - Support the cable from the building structure, not from ceiling grid wires or pipes."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.4 Safety before you touch a fire system","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.4","text":"Working on a live fire system can trigger an evacuation, dispatch the fire department, shut down HVAC, recall elevators, release doors, or discharge a suppression system . Before you do anything that could cause a signal: 1. Call the monitoring center and put the account on test. Get the operator's name. 2. Notify the owner or building contact , plus occupants if appliances will sound. Some jurisdictions also require notifying the fire department. 3. Disable outputs you don't want to operate using the panel's disable or bypass functions, per the panel manual and the building's procedure: releasing circuits (clean agent, preaction, deluge), elevator recall, HVAC shutdown, door unlocking. Never test with a releasing circuit armed unless the test plan calls for a full discharge test with everyone involved. 4. When you're done , re-enable everything you disabled, reset the panel, confirm it's normal with no troubles, and take the account off test with the monitoring center. Other hazards on fire jobs: - 120 VAC is in the panel (and in power supplies and NAC extenders). Turn off and lock the dedicated breaker before working on primary power. - Batteries can deliver very high current. Disconnect them before working in the panel; don't let a wrench or tool bridge the terminals. - Ladders and lifts: most devices are on the ceiling. Use the right ladder, and a lift for high ceilings. - If the system will be out of service for a while, the AHJ may require a fire watch (Module 8). Field tip (David to add): the step new techs skip most often when putting a system on test, and what happens when they do."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"1.5 Who's who on a fire alarm job","where":"Module 1","href":"learn/1/1.5","text":"Role What they do Owner Responsible for keeping the system inspected, tested, and maintained Designer / engineer Produces the drawings, calculations, and sequence of operations Installing contractor Pulls wire, mounts devices, terminates, programs (often you) AHJ / fire inspector Approves plans, witnesses acceptance tests, enforces the code Monitoring center Receives alarm, supervisory, and trouble signals and dispatches Sprinkler contractor Installs waterflow and valve tamper switches you'll monitor Elevator, HVAC, door hardware contractors Their equipment is controlled by the fire alarm; coordinate testing with them A smooth fire job is mostly coordination. Know who you need on site for the final test before you schedule it."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 1: Fire Alarm Foundations","where":"Learn","href":"learn/1","text":"You'll be able to: explain why fire alarm work is held to a higher standard than burglary work, name the codes that govern it, recognize power-limited fire alarm wiring, and follow the safety steps that come before touching a live fire system. These lessons give an overview based on NFPA 72. Codes are adopted and amended locally, and editions change. Always confirm requirements with your company, the AHJ, the adopted code edition, and the equipment's installation manual."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.1 The parts of a system","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.1","text":"Part What it does Fire alarm control unit (FACU / FACP) The brain. Watches every circuit, decides what each signal means, sounds the alarms, runs the control functions, and reports off site Initiating devices Inputs: smoke and heat detectors, manual pull stations, waterflow switches, valve tamper switches, duct detectors Notification appliances Outputs that warn people: horns, strobes, horn/strobes, speakers, chimes Annunciator A remote display (often at the main entrance) so firefighters can see what's in alarm without going to the panel Power Primary (a dedicated 120 VAC branch circuit) and secondary (batteries, sometimes a generator) Communicator Sends signals to the monitoring center (DACT phone lines, cellular, IP, or radio) Control outputs Relays and modules that recall elevators, shut down fans, release door holders, unlock doors, and trigger other systems NAC power extenders Booster power supplies that add notification circuits and power when the panel's own NAC capacity runs out"},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.2 The three signal types","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.2","text":"Every condition a fire panel reports falls into one of three types. Knowing which one you're looking at tells you how urgent it is and who needs to know. Signal Means Examples What happens Alarm A fire may be happening Smoke detector, heat detector, pull station, waterflow Notification appliances sound, control functions run, monitoring center dispatches the fire department Supervisory Something that protects the building is off-normal Sprinkler valve closed (tamper), low air on a dry-pipe system, duct detector (in many systems), fire pump trouble Panel sounds and shows supervisory; monitoring center calls the building to investigate. No evacuation Trouble The fire alarm system itself has a fault Open or ground on a circuit, AC power loss, low battery, missing device, communication failure Panel sounds and shows trouble; monitoring center notifies the service company Some systems also have a pre-alarm (an addressable smoke detector getting close to its alarm threshold) that alerts staff before a full alarm. Alarm signals take priority over supervisory, and supervisory over trouble. Latching: alarm and supervisory signals stay on the panel until someone resets it, even after the device restores. Most troubles clear on their own when the fault is fixed."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.3 Conventional vs addressable","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.3","text":"Conventional (zoned) systems Detectors are wired on initiating device circuits (IDCs) , one circuit per zone, each supervised by an end-of-line (EOL) device at the far end. The panel only knows that something on Zone 3 is in alarm, so someone has to go find which detector. Common in small buildings. Simple and inexpensive, but finding a problem means walking the zone. Addressable (intelligent) systems Every device has its own address and talks to the panel over a signaling line circuit (SLC) , a two-wire data loop. The panel knows exactly which device is in alarm or trouble: \"Smoke detector, 2nd floor corridor outside room 214.\" Addressable systems also: - Report each detector's sensitivity and dirtiness - Let you disable one device instead of a whole zone - Use monitor modules to bring conventional devices (waterflow switches, tampers, older detectors) onto the SLC - Use control/relay modules to run outputs from anywhere on the loop Notification circuits Either type uses notification appliance circuits (NACs) to power horns and strobes, usually at a regulated 24 VDC. The panel reverses polarity on the NAC to sound the appliances, so NAC appliances are polarized and use diodes or EOL resistors for supervision."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.4 The signal path","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.4","text":"1. Detection: a smoke detector senses smoke and sends an alarm (current draw on a conventional IDC, a data message on an addressable SLC). 2. Panel processing: the panel confirms the signal (some smoke systems use alarm verification , which resets and rechecks the detector before going into alarm), then follows its programming. 3. Occupant notification: NACs activate; horns sound the temporal-3 evacuation pattern and strobes flash (Module 4). 4. Control functions: elevators recall, fans shut down, door holders release, according to the sequence of operations . 5. Off-site reporting: the communicator transmits to the monitoring center, which dispatches the fire department. 6. Annunciation: the panel and annunciator display the device and location so the responding firefighters can go straight to it. Silencing the horns does not reset the system. The panel stays in alarm until the cause is cleared and the panel is reset."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"2.5 Monitoring and communication","where":"Module 2","href":"learn/2/2.5","text":"Most commercial fire systems are monitored by a supervising station : a central station, a proprietary station (owned by the building), or a remote station. - DACT (digital alarm communicator transmitter): sends signals over phone lines. Traditionally needs two separate communication paths. - Cellular and IP communicators: increasingly the primary path as copper phone lines disappear. Many single-path cellular or IP communicators are now listed as an acceptable sole means when the path itself is supervised. - Radio: private radio networks in some areas. The panel must supervise its communication path and report a trouble if it can't get through. Test signals go to the monitoring center on a regular schedule (commonly at least every 24 hours) so a dead path gets noticed."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 2: How a Fire Alarm System Works","where":"Learn","href":"learn/2","text":"You'll be able to: name the parts of a fire alarm system, tell the three signal types apart, explain conventional vs addressable systems, and trace a signal from a detector to the fire department."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.1 Smoke detectors","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.1","text":"Photoelectric detectors shine a light inside a chamber; smoke scatters the light onto a sensor. They respond best to smoldering fires with large, visible smoke particles (a cigarette in a couch, an overheated wire). Most new commercial spot detectors are photoelectric. Ionization detectors use a tiny radioactive source to make the air in a chamber conduct a small current; smoke particles disrupt it. They respond faster to fast, flaming fires with small particles. They contain a radioactive source, so follow the manufacturer's disposal instructions; don't throw them in the trash. Multi-criteria detectors combine photoelectric with heat, CO, or infrared sensing to respond faster and reject nuisance sources such as steam and cooking. Where smoke detectors don't belong (they'll false alarm or fail early): - Kitchens, bathrooms with showers, garages, and other dusty, steamy, or exhaust-filled spaces - Places outside the detector's listed temperature and humidity range, commonly about 32 °F to 100 °F and up to 93% relative humidity - Directly in the airflow from supply diffusers or near return grilles (Module 7) Use a heat detector in those spaces instead, when the design allows. Two-wire vs four-wire: on conventional systems, two-wire smoke detectors get power and signal on the same two IDC wires and must be listed as compatible with that panel's zone. Four-wire detectors use two wires for the alarm contact and two for resettable power, with an EOL power supervision relay at the end of the power run so the panel knows if power is lost."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.2 Heat detectors","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.2","text":"Heat detectors respond to heat, not smoke. They're slower but nearly immune to nuisance alarms, so they're used where smoke detectors would false alarm. A heat detector is property protection; it doesn't replace a smoke detector where smoke detection is required for life safety. Type How it works Fixed temperature Alarms when the air reaches a set temperature, commonly 135 °F for ordinary spaces or 194 °F for hot spaces like attics and boiler rooms Rate-of-rise (ROR) Alarms when temperature climbs fast, commonly about 15 °F per minute , even if it hasn't reached the fixed setting Combination Fixed temperature plus rate-of-rise in one head Rate-compensated Accounts for how fast the air is heating so it responds close to the actual set point Linear heat cable A cable that detects heat anywhere along its length (tunnels, conveyors, cable trays) - Pick the rating for the space: the detector's temperature rating should be at least 20 °F above the highest ceiling temperature the space normally reaches. - Restorable vs non-restorable: some fixed-temperature heads use a fusible element that melts and must be replaced after an alarm. Check before you test with a heat gun; you can't test a non-restorable head that way. - Rate-of-rise heads can false alarm in spaces that heat up fast on purpose (near ovens, loading dock doors in winter). Use fixed temperature there. - Heat detectors have a listed spacing (often 50 ft or less) that's reduced for higher ceilings. Use the spacing on the detector's listing, not the smoke detector spacing."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.3 Manual pull stations","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.3","text":"A manual fire alarm box (pull station) lets a person start the alarm. - Single-action: pull the handle. Dual-action: push or lift a flap, then pull. Dual-action, or a protective cover, helps where malicious pulls are a problem. Covers that sound a local horn when lifted are common in schools. - Location: within 5 feet of each exit doorway on each floor. - Height: the operable part between 42 and 48 inches above the floor. - Travel distance: no more than 200 feet of travel to the nearest pull station on the same floor. - Color: red, unless the AHJ approves otherwise. - Reset: most pull stations stay down until reset with a key or tool, so you can tell which one was pulled. Some building codes don't require pull stations throughout a fully sprinklered building (often only one, at a location the AHJ approves). Follow the approved drawings."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.4 Sprinkler waterflow and valve supervision","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.4","text":"Most fire alarm systems monitor the building's sprinkler system. Waterflow switches (alarm) A vane-type switch sits in the sprinkler pipe; water flowing past pushes the vane and closes the contact. A pressure switch is used on dry-pipe and some other systems. - Retard (delay): waterflow switches have a built-in adjustable delay so pressure surges don't cause false alarms. The signal must still come in within 90 seconds of flow equal to one sprinkler head. - Waterflow is an alarm signal. It means water is moving, which usually means a sprinkler head opened. - Test it by flowing water through the inspector's test valve , not by tripping the switch by hand, so you test the whole path. Coordinate with the sprinkler contractor or building engineer. Valve supervisory switches (tamper switches) A tamper switch mounts on a sprinkler control valve and reports when the valve is moved toward closed. - Must signal within two revolutions of the handwheel, or when the valve has moved one-fifth of its travel from fully open. - Must restore only when the valve is fully open again. - It's a supervisory signal, not an alarm: a closed valve means the sprinklers may not work. Never wire a tamper switch on the same circuit or zone as a waterflow switch , or a closed valve could look like a fire (or a fire could be missed). Supervisory and alarm signals must be distinct at the panel. Field tip (David to add): how you set the retard on a waterflow switch, and the setting you use most."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"3.5 Other initiating devices","where":"Module 3","href":"learn/3/3.5","text":"- Duct smoke detectors: sample air in an HVAC duct to shut down the air handler and stop it spreading smoke. Covered in Module 7. - Projected beam detectors: a transmitter and receiver (or reflector) across a large open space like a warehouse or atrium. Alignment matters; building movement can cause troubles. - Air sampling (aspirating) detectors: pipes pull air back to a very sensitive detector. Used in data centers, clean rooms, and places where you can't reach the ceiling to service spot detectors. - Flame detectors: see the infrared or ultraviolet light of a flame. Used for fuel and chemical hazards. - Carbon monoxide (CO) detectors: detect CO from fuel-burning appliances. CO is a separate signal from fire, with its own notification pattern (Module 4). - Monitor modules: let an addressable system watch any dry contact (waterflow, tamper, fire pump, suppression panel)."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 3: Initiating Devices","where":"Learn","href":"learn/3","text":"You'll be able to: pick the right detector for a space, explain how each type senses fire, place pull stations, and connect sprinkler waterflow and valve supervisory switches correctly."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.1 Audible signals and the temporal-3 pattern","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.1","text":"Every fire evacuation signal in the US uses the same rhythm so people recognize it anywhere: the temporal-3 pattern. Three half-second pulses, then a pause, repeating every 4 seconds. - Horns produce the pattern from the NAC (the panel or a sync module sets the rhythm). - Chimes and bells are used in some occupancies; they still follow the evacuation pattern. - Carbon monoxide uses a different pattern, temporal-4 (four short beeps, then a pause), so people can tell CO from fire."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.2 How loud is loud enough","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.2","text":"Audibility is measured in dBA with a sound meter, 5 feet above the floor . Mode Requirement Public mode (everyone evacuates) At least 15 dB above the average ambient sound level, or 5 dB above the maximum sound level lasting 60 seconds or more, whichever is greater Private mode (staff only, such as a hospital) At least 10 dB above average ambient , or 5 dB above the maximum lasting 60 seconds Maximum No more than 110 dBA at the minimum hearing distance Sleeping areas (hotels, dorms, apartments) need more: - At least 15 dB above average ambient, 5 dB above the maximum lasting 60 seconds, or 75 dBA at the pillow , whichever is greater. - A low-frequency (520 Hz) tone , because it wakes sleeping people, including those with some hearing loss, far better than a high-pitched horn. Doors, carpets, and walls cut sound a lot. A horn in the corridor rarely gets 75 dBA to a pillow behind a closed door, so sleeping rooms usually get their own appliance. Field tip (David to add): the most common reason a building fails audibility at the acceptance test."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.3 Strobes and candela","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.3","text":"Strobes (visible notification) alert people who are deaf or hard of hearing, and anyone in a noisy space. Brightness is rated in candela (cd) . Many strobes are multi-candela : you set 15, 30, 75, 110, and so on with a switch, and the current draw goes up with the setting. Wall mounting: the entire lens between 80 and 96 inches above the floor. Flash rate: between 1 and 2 flashes per second . Room spacing (one wall-mounted strobe per room, centered on a wall): Room size Minimum candela 20 × 20 ft 15 cd 28 × 28 ft 30 cd 40 × 40 ft 60 cd 45 × 45 ft 75 cd 54 × 54 ft 95 cd 55 × 55 ft 115 cd Ceiling-mounted strobes have their own table that depends on ceiling height. Two or more strobes can cover a larger room. Use the values on the approved drawings. Corridors (20 ft wide or less): strobes within 15 ft of each end and no more than 100 ft apart , with a minimum of 15 cd. Sleeping rooms: 177 cd if the strobe is within 24 inches of the ceiling, 110 cd if it's 24 inches or more below the ceiling."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.4 Synchronization","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.4","text":"Flashing strobes out of step can trigger seizures in people with photosensitive epilepsy. When more than two strobes can be seen from any one spot, they must flash in sync . - Sync comes from the panel's NAC (built-in sync protocol) or from a sync module . - Strobes and horns from different manufacturers usually don't sync with each other. Match the sync protocol to the appliances. - NAC power extenders must use the same sync protocol and be set to follow the panel, or the strobes on the extender won't match the ones on the panel. - Horn/strobes on a two-wire sync circuit can silence the horns while strobes keep flashing (common after the alarm is acknowledged, if the AHJ allows)."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"4.5 Voice evacuation","where":"Module 4","href":"learn/4/4.5","text":"Emergency voice/alarm communication systems (EVACS) use speakers instead of horns. They play the temporal-3 tone, then a recorded message (\"A fire emergency has been reported in the building. Please leave by the nearest exit...\"). Firefighters can also make live announcements. - Required in many high-rises, assembly occupancies, and some schools, per the building code. - Speakers run on 25 V or 70.7 V audio circuits from an amplifier, with taps (such as ¼, ½, 1, 2 W) that set loudness. - Intelligibility matters, not just loudness: people must understand the words. Echoes and hard surfaces make that hard, so speaker layout is engineered. - Voice systems need more battery: 15 minutes of alarm at full load instead of 5. - Some voice systems are mass notification systems (MNS) that can also announce weather, lockdown, or other emergencies."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 4: Notification Appliances","where":"Learn","href":"learn/4","text":"You'll be able to: explain the evacuation signal, check that horns are loud enough, choose and place strobes by candela, keep strobes synchronized, and recognize voice evacuation systems."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.1 The three circuit types","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.1","text":"Circuit Carries Typical wiring IDC (initiating device circuit) Conventional detectors, pull stations, waterflow and tamper switches 2 wires, EOL resistor at the last device SLC (signaling line circuit) Addressable devices and modules, panel-to-panel networks 2 wires (often twisted, sometimes shielded) NAC (notification appliance circuit) Horns, strobes, speakers 2 wires, EOL resistor at the last appliance, usually 24 VDC The wire size, length limit, and whether shielding is needed come from the panel's installation manual . SLC limits are often given as a maximum loop resistance and capacitance, not just a length."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.2 Class B and Class A","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.2","text":"NFPA 72 defines circuits by how they behave when something goes wrong , using classes. Class B A single path out to the devices, ending at an EOL device. - An open anywhere gives a trouble , and every device past the break stops working . - A ground fault gives a trouble. - Most common: it's cheaper and simpler. Class A The circuit goes out to every device and returns to the panel on a separate pair. There's no EOL; the panel supervises the loop itself. - A single open gives a trouble , but every device still works , because the panel feeds the circuit from both ends. - A ground fault gives a trouble. - Required by the design in some buildings, such as high-rises or where the engineer specifies it. The outgoing and return conductors don't run in the same cable or raceway , so one damaged cable can't take out both. The exceptions are limited, such as short drops to a device or into the panel; the drawings and the code edition in force say how far. Class X Like Class A, but it also keeps working through a short (using isolation modules between groups of devices). Common on addressable SLCs in larger buildings. Class N Ethernet-based pathways for newer networked and voice systems. You'll see it on newer designs."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.3 T-taps and why they're restricted","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.3","text":"A T-tap is a branch off the middle of a circuit. - Conventional IDCs and NACs: no T-taps. The EOL at the end only supervises wire that runs through it. A branch is unsupervised: if it breaks, the panel never knows, and those devices are silently dead. - Class A circuits: no T-taps. A branch breaks the loop design. - Class B addressable SLCs: T-taps usually allowed if the panel manufacturer permits them, because the panel polls every device and notices one that stops answering. Check the manual for limits on total wire length with taps. Wire through every device. On conventional circuits, the wire must land on the device terminals in and out (or on separate terminals, as the device instructions show), not be twisted together with a pigtail to the device. That way, removing a device opens the circuit and the panel reports a trouble."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.4 Reading a fire circuit with a meter","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.4","text":"Disconnect the circuit from the panel first (with the panel and account on test). Don't meter a circuit while it's connected; you'll read the panel, not the wire. Conventional IDC with EOL: You read Means About the EOL value Wiring good (with all devices normal) Open (OL) Break in the wire, loose terminal, or missing device or EOL Near 0 Ω Short, or a device in alarm Any reading from either wire to ground Ground fault Two-wire smoke detectors have electronics across the circuit. They're polarized, so you may read different values depending on which way your leads are. Read in both directions, and expect the EOL value in one of them. NAC with EOL: appliances have built-in blocking diodes, so in one direction you read about the EOL value and in the other direction you read lower (the appliances). An open in one direction means a break; a near-zero reading in both directions means a short. Never use a megohmmeter (insulation tester) with devices or the panel connected. The test voltage will destroy devices. Disconnect every device first, or don't megger at all."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.5 Ground faults","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.5","text":"A ground fault is any connection between a fire alarm conductor and earth ground: a staple through the cable, a wire touching a metal box, a damp splice. Fire panels check for ground faults on all circuits and report a trouble . A single ground fault usually doesn't stop the system from working, but a second one on another wire can create a short or an unwanted path, so every ground fault must be fixed . Finding a ground fault: 1. Note which circuit the panel names (many panels show the circuit or even polarity). 2. If it doesn't, disconnect circuits one at a time until the trouble clears. Wait for the panel to re-check between steps; it can take a minute. 3. On the faulted circuit, meter from each wire to earth ground with the circuit off the panel. 4. Split the circuit at an accessible device and test each half to narrow it down. 5. Check devices in damp spaces, exterior devices, and anything mounted in metal back boxes first. Field tip (David to add): your fastest way to track a ground fault on a big addressable loop."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"5.6 NAC power, voltage drop, and extenders","where":"Module 5","href":"learn/5/5.6","text":"NACs carry the heaviest current on the system. Strobes draw the most, especially at high candela settings. - Each NAC has a maximum current rating (often 1.5 to 3 A per circuit). Add up every appliance on the circuit at its listed current. - Voltage drop matters more on NACs than anywhere else. Appliances listed for \"regulated 24 VDC\" typically work down to about 16 VDC . - Calculate drop from the battery voltage at the end of standby , not a fresh 24 or 27 V. A common starting value is 20.4 VDC (85% of 24 V). - Use the appliance's current at its minimum operating voltage if the manufacturer lists it. Strobes draw more current as voltage drops. - When a NAC is too long or too loaded: use heavier wire, split the circuit, or add a NAC power extender near the appliances. Extenders have their own batteries and must be supervised by the panel. Use the NAC Voltage Drop calculator ."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 5: Circuits and Wiring","where":"Learn","href":"learn/5","text":"You'll be able to: wire IDCs, SLCs, and NACs in Class B and Class A, explain why T-taps are allowed on some circuits and not others, read a fire circuit with a meter, and find a ground fault."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.1 Primary power","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.1","text":"The fire alarm panel's main power comes from a dedicated branch circuit : a breaker that feeds the fire alarm and nothing else (other fire alarm equipment, like NAC extenders, can share it if the design allows). The breaker must be: - Marked in red and identified as \"FIRE ALARM CIRCUIT\" - Locked or otherwise protected so it can't be turned off by accident, and accessible only to authorized people - Recorded at the panel : the location of the panel board and breaker number is written at the fire alarm control unit No plug-in transformers and no GFCI or AFCI breakers on fire alarm primary power unless the manufacturer and AHJ allow it. A nuisance trip would take the system to battery without anyone noticing until the battery dies. An electrician usually installs the branch circuit. Check it before you terminate: correct breaker, red marking, lock, and a panel label."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.2 Secondary power (batteries)","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.2","text":"When AC fails, batteries take over automatically with no loss of signals. The batteries must run the system for: Requirement Typical value Standby 24 hours of normal (non-alarm) operation Then alarm (horns and strobes) 5 minutes with every notification appliance operating Then alarm (voice evacuation) 15 minutes at maximum connected load - Battery calculations add a 20% safety margin on top of the calculated amp-hours. - A generator can supplement batteries, but the batteries still need to carry the system for at least the transfer time, and the exact standby required with a generator depends on the code edition and AHJ. - Most panels use two 12 V sealed lead-acid batteries in series for 24 V. Both must be the same size, age, and brand. - The panel's charger can only charge a battery up to a certain size. Check the panel's maximum battery before specifying a big one; use an external charger if the manual allows. Use the Fire Battery calculator . The formula: Required Ah = [(standby current in A × 24 h) + (alarm current in A × alarm hours)] × 1.2 Alarm hours: 5 minutes = 0.083 h; 15 minutes = 0.25 h."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.3 Power troubles and what they mean","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.3","text":"Panel shows Usually means AC loss / AC trouble Breaker off or tripped, wiring fault, blown panel fuse Low battery Batteries discharged, failing, or charger problem Battery trouble / missing Battery disconnected, loose lead, blown battery fuse Ground fault A field wire or the power wiring touching ground Charger trouble Panel's charger circuit has failed AC loss reporting delay: the panel reports AC loss locally right away, but the transmission to the monitoring center is delayed (commonly 1 to 3 hours ) so short outages don't flood the monitoring center."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.4 Battery care and replacement","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.4","text":"- Date every battery with the month and year of manufacture or installation when you install it. - Sealed lead-acid batteries are commonly replaced every 5 years (or sooner per the manufacturer or if they fail a test). - The charger must be able to recharge a fully discharged battery within 48 hours . - At inspection, batteries are checked visually, voltage-checked under load, and load or capacity tested on the schedule in NFPA 72. A battery that reads full voltage with no load can still collapse under load. - Replace both batteries together. A new battery in series with an old one will be dragged down. - Keep batteries off concrete floors in cold areas and away from heat; temperature shortens battery life. Safe battery handling: disconnect the battery leads before AC when powering down, and reconnect AC before batteries when powering up (or follow the panel manual's order). Don't let tools bridge the terminals. Recycle old batteries; don't throw them out. Field tip (David to add): what you write on a battery when you install it, and where."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"6.5 NAC power extenders and remote power supplies","where":"Module 6","href":"learn/6/6.5","text":"When the panel can't power all the NACs or devices, a NAC power extender (booster) or remote power supply is added. - It has its own primary power (dedicated branch circuit, marked and locked like the panel's) and its own batteries . Include it in the battery calculations. - It's triggered by the panel's NAC or a control module, and it reports its troubles (AC loss, low battery, ground fault, NAC trouble) back to the panel. An unsupervised power supply isn't acceptable. - Set its sync to match the panel (Module 4). - Label it with its circuit and what it powers."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 6: Power Supplies and Batteries","where":"Learn","href":"learn/6","text":"You'll be able to: set up primary power the way the code expects, size standby batteries, recognize power troubles, and maintain batteries so they're ready when the power fails."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.1 Working from the drawings","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.1","text":"Fire alarm work starts with approved drawings : the plans the AHJ reviewed. They show every device, its type and setting (candela, temperature, address), the circuits, the panel location, and a sequence of operations (what happens when each device activates). - Install what's on the drawings. If a device can't go where it's shown (a duct, a beam, a light fixture in the way), get the change approved by the designer. Small moves can change coverage and break the approval. - Keep a set of red-line drawings on site. Mark every change, the real wire routes, and device addresses. These become the as-built (record) drawings. - Check the edition of NFPA 72 and local amendments listed on the drawings."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.2 Smoke detector placement","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.2","text":"These are the basic rules for spot-type smoke detectors on smooth, flat ceilings . Beams, joists, sloped ceilings, and high ceilings change the rules. Follow the drawings. - Spacing: a nominal 30 ft spacing between detectors, and no more than 15 ft from a wall. - Every point on the ceiling must be within 21 ft of a detector (0.7 × the 30 ft spacing). This matters in odd-shaped rooms and long narrow corridors. - Ceiling or wall: on the ceiling, or on a sidewall with the top of the detector within 12 inches of the ceiling. - Air movement: keep detectors at least 3 ft from supply air diffusers and return air openings. Air blowing across a detector keeps smoke out of it, and dust from a supply vent makes it dirty fast. - Not in a dead-air spot: avoid the very peak of a pitched ceiling and the corner where wall meets ceiling, per the device instructions. - Access: leave room to service and test it. A detector in an unreachable spot won't get tested. Residential smoke alarms (stand-alone, household) follow the household chapter of NFPA 72, which keeps a 4-inch rule: on the ceiling at least 4 inches from the wall, or on the wall 4 to 12 inches below the ceiling."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.3 Heat detector placement","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.3","text":"- Use the listed spacing for that detector (on the label or instructions), for example 50 × 50 ft. - Reduce spacing for high ceilings: the listed spacing assumes a ceiling about 10 ft high; above that, a reduction factor applies. - Every point on the ceiling within 0.7 × the listed spacing of a detector, as for smoke. - Choose the temperature rating for the hottest normal ceiling temperature (Module 3)."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.4 Duct smoke detectors","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.4","text":"Duct detectors stop an air handler from spreading smoke through the building. They don't replace area smoke detectors. - Where required: NFPA 90A and the mechanical code call for them on air handlers over a certain size. A commonly cited trigger is supply systems over 2,000 CFM , detecting downstream of the filters. Larger systems (commonly over 15,000 CFM ) may also need return-side detection. Follow the mechanical drawings. - Signal type: a duct detector is usually set up as a supervisory signal that shuts down the fan, rather than a building-wide alarm, unless the AHJ or design calls for an alarm. - Installation: sampling tube across the duct with holes facing the airflow, in a straight section where air isn't turbulent (often 6 to 10 duct widths from bends or dampers, per the instructions). Check the duct's air velocity is within the detector's listed range. - Remote test station or indicator: required where the detector isn't easy to see and reach, so it can be tested and its alarm LED seen from the floor. - Test it with the manufacturer's method (smoke through the test port, or a magnet test for function only, plus an airflow check across the sampling tubes). Field tip (David to add): the mistake you see most often on duct detector installs."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.5 Control functions: elevators, HVAC, doors","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.5","text":"The fire alarm often controls other systems. These are installed per the sequence of operations and tested with the other trades. Elevator recall (Phase I): - Smoke detectors in each elevator lobby, the machine room, and the hoistway (when required) recall elevators to the main floor, or to an alternate floor if the main lobby detector is the one in alarm. - Shunt trip: where sprinklers are in an elevator machine room or hoistway, heat detectors within 2 ft of each sprinkler head , with a lower temperature rating and faster response than the sprinkler, disconnect elevator power before water flows. - The fire alarm provides dry contacts to the elevator controller; the elevator contractor wires their side. Test together. Control relays: relays or control modules for emergency control functions (HVAC shutdown, elevator recall, door release) go within 3 ft of the controlled device, with the wiring to the relay supervised. HVAC shutdown: the fire alarm relay stops the air handler (and closes smoke dampers where the design calls for it) per the sequence of operations. Door holders and locks: magnetic door holders release fire doors on alarm. Electrically locked doors on egress routes unlock on alarm per the building code. Coordinate with the door hardware installer."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"7.6 Panels, annunciators, and construction protection","where":"Module 7","href":"learn/7/7.6","text":"- Panel location: where the drawings show, usually near the main entrance or a fire command center, with working space in front. - Annunciator: at the entrance firefighters will use, as approved by the AHJ. - Label everything: circuits in the panel, device addresses on the devices (where allowed), and a zone or point list in the panel. - Protect detectors during construction: smoke detectors installed before construction cleaning is finished get filled with drywall dust. Don't install heads until the area is clean, or cover them with the manufacturer's dust covers and remove every cover before the system goes into service. A covered detector can't see smoke."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 7: Installation and Device Placement","where":"Learn","href":"learn/7","text":"You'll be able to: read a fire alarm drawing, place smoke and heat detectors correctly, install duct detectors, wire elevator and HVAC control functions, and protect devices during construction."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.1 The acceptance test","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.1","text":"Before a new system (or a big change) goes into service, it gets a 100% acceptance test : every device and every function, often witnessed by the AHJ. 1. Pre-test it yourself first. Never show an inspector a system you haven't already tested completely. 2. Test every initiating device : each one causes the right signal, at the right address or zone, with the right label. 3. Test every notification appliance : sounds and flashes, sync is correct, audibility is measured where required. 4. Test every control function against the sequence of operations: elevator recall, HVAC shutdown, door release, with the other trades present. 5. Test circuit supervision : open, ground, and (for Class A or X) shorts on each circuit produce the right trouble. 6. Test power : AC loss, battery operation, battery calculation checked against actual current. 7. Test off-site reporting : alarm, supervisory, and trouble signals reach the monitoring center with the right account and point. 8. Fill out the Record of Completion and give the owner the documentation (Lesson 8.5)."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.2 Inspection and testing schedule","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.2","text":"After acceptance, the owner is responsible for periodic inspection, testing, and maintenance. Common frequencies: Item Common frequency Control panel, trouble signals, power Visual inspection semiannually or annually, functional test annually Smoke, heat (restorable), duct detectors, pull stations Functional test annually Notification appliances Functional test annually Waterflow and valve supervisory switches Semiannually Batteries (sealed lead-acid) Inspect and test semiannually , replace about every 5 years Monitoring transmission Tested annually , plus automatic test signals daily or more often Smoke detector sensitivity See Lesson 8.3 Sprinkler-related devices are often also on the sprinkler inspection schedule (NFPA 25); coordinate with the sprinkler inspector so a valve closed for their test doesn't surprise you."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.3 How to test each device","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.3","text":"Device Test method Smoke detector Listed aerosol smoke or a smoke generator that actually puts smoke into the chamber. A magnet test only checks the electronics, not the chamber. Smoke sensitivity Measure with the panel's sensitivity readout (addressable) or a listed sensitivity tester. Required within 1 year after install, then every other year . If results stay in range, the interval can be extended up to 5 years . Heat detector (restorable) Listed heat source (heat gun or cup tester), within the manufacturer's guidelines. Don't overheat plastic housings. Heat detector (non-restorable) Not heat tested; test the circuit with a mechanical or electrical method. Replaced or sample-tested after a set number of years per NFPA 72. Pull station Operate it as a person would, then reset it. Duct detector Smoke through the test port or as the manufacturer directs, plus confirm airflow in the sampling tubes. Waterflow switch Flow water through the inspector's test valve; time the signal. Tamper switch Close the valve (with the sprinkler contractor or building), confirm the signal by two turns, open fully, confirm restore. Horn/strobe Activate and confirm every appliance works and sync looks right. Never use an open flame to test a detector. Field tip (David to add): how you keep track of which devices you've tested on a large building."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.4 Impairments, fire watch, and nuisance alarms","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.4","text":"An impairment is any time the system (or part of it) can't do its job: a circuit disabled, a panel down for repair, devices removed during a remodel. - Notify the AHJ and the owner when the system will be out of service for more than 4 hours in a 24-hour period , and the monitoring center whenever signals will be affected. - The AHJ may require a fire watch : trained people walking the building looking for fire, with a way to call the fire department, until the system is restored. - Tag the panel with what's impaired and who to call, and remove the tag when you restore it. - Never leave a site with a circuit, device, or output disabled unless the owner and AHJ know and the impairment is documented. Nuisance alarms (an alarm caused by something other than fire, like dust, steam, or a bad device) erode trust in the system and get people to ignore real alarms. Fix the cause: clean or replace the detector, move it per the design, or change the detector type with the designer's approval. Don't fix a nuisance alarm by disabling the device and leaving it."},{"kind":"Lesson","title":"8.5 Documentation","where":"Module 8","href":"learn/8/8.5","text":"Every fire job produces paperwork. Leave it complete; the next tech and the inspector depend on it. - Record of Completion: the form (from NFPA 72) describing the system, its power, circuits, devices, and monitoring, signed at acceptance. - Record (as-built) drawings, sequence of operations, battery and voltage drop calculations, and manufacturer manuals: kept on site, often in a documentation cabinet at the panel. - Inspection and test reports: a record of each device tested, the result, and any deficiencies. Retained at least until the next test plus one year. - Site-specific software and passwords: a copy of the panel program kept on site or with the owner, so the system can be serviced if the original installer is gone."},{"kind":"Module","title":"Module 8: Testing, Inspection, and Maintenance","where":"Learn","href":"learn/8","text":"You'll be able to: run an acceptance test, follow the inspection and testing schedule, test each device type the right way, handle an impaired system, and leave the paperwork the AHJ expects. NFPA 72's testing chapter has a detailed table of methods and frequencies. This module covers the common items. The edition the AHJ has adopted and the manufacturer's instructions always win."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Signal types at a glance","where":"Reference","href":"reference/signal-types-at-a-glance","text":"Signal Means Examples Monitoring center Alarm Possible fire Smoke, heat, pull station, waterflow Dispatches fire department Supervisory Fire protection off-normal Valve tamper, low air, duct detector (usually), fire pump Calls the building Trouble Fire alarm system fault Open, ground, AC loss, low battery, missing device Calls the service company Priority: alarm, then supervisory, then trouble. Alarm and supervisory latch until reset."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Before you test","where":"Reference","href":"reference/before-you-test","text":"1. Call the monitoring center and put the account on test . Get the operator's name. 2. Notify the owner or building contact, plus occupants if appliances will sound. Some jurisdictions also require notifying the fire department. 3. Disable releasing circuits (suppression) and any output you don't want to run: elevator recall, HVAC shutdown, door unlock. 4. Coordinate with sprinkler, elevator, and HVAC trades if their equipment is part of the test. 5. When finished: re-enable everything, reset, panel normal with no troubles , account off test with the operator."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Circuit classes","where":"Reference","href":"reference/circuit-classes","text":"- No T-taps on conventional IDCs, NACs, or any Class A circuit. - T-taps on Class B addressable SLC only if the manufacturer allows. - Class A outgoing and return never in the same cable or raceway, except limited short runs such as drops to a device or into the panel."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Meter readings on fire circuits","where":"Reference","href":"reference/meter-readings-on-fire-circuits","text":"Circuit disconnected from the panel, panel and account on test. Reading Conventional IDC NAC (appliances polarized) ≈ EOL value Good Good (in the reverse direction) Lower than EOL one way, EOL the other Normal for two-wire smokes Normal (appliances seen forward) OL in both directions Open: wire, terminal, missing device or EOL Open ≈ 0 Ω both directions Short, or a device in alarm Short Any reading to earth ground Ground fault Ground fault No megohmmeter with devices or the panel connected."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Smoke detector placement","where":"Reference","href":"reference/smoke-detector-placement","text":"Smooth flat ceiling, spot-type. Follow the drawings for beams, slopes, and high ceilings. - 30 ft nominal spacing, no more than 15 ft from a wall - Every ceiling point within 21 ft of a detector - Ceiling, or sidewall with the top of detector within 12 in of the ceiling - Household smoke alarms: 4 in from the wall on the ceiling, or 4 to 12 in down on the wall - At least 3 ft from supply diffusers and return openings - Not in kitchens, showers, garages, dusty spaces, or outside the listed range (commonly 32 to 100 °F, up to 93% humidity) - Dust covers during construction; remove every one before service"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Heat detector quick rules","where":"Reference","href":"reference/heat-detector-quick-rules","text":"- Rating at least 20 °F above the hottest normal ceiling temperature - Common ratings: 135 °F ordinary, 194 °F hot spaces - Rate-of-rise: commonly about 15 °F per minute - Use the listed spacing ; reduce it above about 10 ft ceilings - Every point within 0.7 × listed spacing - Non-restorable heads can't be heat tested - Heat detectors are property protection, not a substitute for required smoke detection"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Pull stations, waterflow, tamper","where":"Reference","href":"reference/pull-stations-waterflow-tamper","text":"Pull stations - Within 5 ft of each exit doorway, each floor - Operable part 42 to 48 in above the floor - No more than 200 ft travel to the nearest one Waterflow (alarm) - Signal within 90 s of flow equal to one sprinkler - Test through the inspector's test valve Tamper / valve supervisory (supervisory) - Signal within 2 turns of the handwheel or 1/5 of valve travel - Restore only when fully open - Never on the same zone as waterflow"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Strobe candela and placement","where":"Reference","href":"reference/strobe-candela-and-placement","text":"- Wall mount: entire lens 80 to 96 in above the floor - Flash rate 1 to 2 per second - More than 2 visible from one spot: must be synchronized Room (one wall strobe) Minimum cd 20 × 20 ft 15 28 × 28 ft 30 40 × 40 ft 60 45 × 45 ft 75 54 × 54 ft 95 55 × 55 ft 115 - Corridors up to 20 ft wide: 15 cd minimum, within 15 ft of each end, no more than 100 ft apart - Sleeping rooms: 177 cd within 24 in of the ceiling, 110 cd if 24 in or more below"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Audibility and tones","where":"Reference","href":"reference/audibility-and-tones","text":"Measured in dBA, 5 ft above the floor. Mode Minimum Public 15 dB over average ambient, or 5 dB over 60 s max (greater) Private 10 dB over average ambient, or 5 dB over 60 s max Sleeping Greater of 15 dB over ambient, 5 dB over max, or 75 dBA at the pillow; 520 Hz low-frequency tone Maximum 110 dBA"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Secondary power (batteries)","where":"Reference","href":"reference/secondary-power-batteries","text":"Required Ah = [(standby A × 24 h) + (alarm A × alarm h)] × 1.2 Item Value Standby 24 h Alarm, horns/strobes 5 min (0.083 h) Alarm, voice evacuation 15 min (0.25 h) Safety margin 20% Replace sealed lead-acid About every 5 years, date every battery Recharge time Within 48 h - Two 12 V batteries in series for 24 V; same size, age, and brand - Check the panel's maximum battery size and charger capability - Include NAC extenders and power supplies (each has its own batteries) Use the Fire Battery calculator."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"NAC voltage drop","where":"Reference","href":"reference/nac-voltage-drop","text":"- Start from 20.4 V (battery at end of standby), not 24 V - Regulated 24 V appliances commonly work down to 16 V - Use appliance current at minimum voltage when listed - NAC rating commonly 1.5 to 3 A per circuit; check the panel Drop = 2 × one-way length (ft) × total current (A) × Ω per foot AWG Ω per foot 18 0.00639 16 0.00402 14 0.00253 12 0.00159 Lumping all current at the far end is the conservative method. If it fails, the manufacturer's point-to-point calculation may still pass. Use the NAC Voltage Drop calculator."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Primary power","where":"Reference","href":"reference/primary-power","text":"- Dedicated branch circuit, fire alarm only - Breaker marked red, \"FIRE ALARM CIRCUIT\", locked, location recorded at the panel - No GFCI or AFCI unless the manufacturer and AHJ allow - AC loss to monitoring delayed, commonly 1 to 3 h"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Fire alarm cable","where":"Reference","href":"reference/fire-alarm-cable","text":"Marking Use FPL General FPLR Riser (and anywhere FPL is allowed) FPLP Plenum (anywhere) CI Circuit integrity, survivability - Identify fire alarm circuits at terminal and junction locations (red covers and cable are common; red itself isn't required) - Keep separate from power and non-power-limited circuits - Support from structure, not ceiling grid wires - Wire size and length limits: panel manual"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Inspection and testing frequencies","where":"Reference","href":"reference/inspection-and-testing-frequencies","text":"Item Common frequency Smoke, heat, duct detectors, pull stations Annual functional test Horns, strobes, speakers Annual Panel and power, trouble signals Annual Waterflow, tamper Semiannual Batteries Semiannual inspection and test Smoke sensitivity 1 year after install, then every other year; up to 5 years if stable Impairment over 4 h in 24 h Notify AHJ; fire watch may be required"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Contact ID fire event codes","where":"Reference","href":"reference/contact-id-fire-event-codes","text":"Code Event 110 Fire alarm 111 Smoke 113 Waterflow 114 Heat 115 Pull station 116 Duct 200 Fire supervisory 203 Gate valve (tamper) 301 AC loss 302 Low system battery 373 Fire trouble 602 Periodic test Qualifier 1 = new event, 3 = restore. Confirm the panel reports the specific code, not just 110, if the monitoring center needs device type."},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Before you leave the job","where":"Reference","href":"reference/before-you-leave-the-job","text":"- [ ] Every disabled device, circuit, and output re-enabled - [ ] Panel reset, normal, no troubles, no ground faults - [ ] Account off test, confirmed with the operator - [ ] AC and batteries good; batteries dated - [ ] Impairment tags removed (or impairment documented with owner and AHJ) - [ ] Dust covers removed - [ ] Test report filled out; deficiencies listed - [ ] Documentation cabinet updated"},{"kind":"Reference","title":"Glossary","where":"Reference","href":"reference/glossary","text":"- AHJ: Authority Having Jurisdiction; usually the fire marshal. - Alarm verification: the panel resets a smoke detector and rechecks before declaring an alarm. - Annunciator: remote display that shows what's in alarm. - Candela (cd): brightness rating of a strobe. - Class A / B / X: how a circuit behaves with an open, ground, or short. - DACT: digital alarm communicator transmitter; sends signals over phone lines. - dBA: sound level, A-weighted, used for audibility. - EVACS: emergency voice/alarm communication system. - FACP / FACU: fire alarm control panel / unit. - Fire watch: people patrolling for fire while a system is impaired. - FPL / FPLR / FPLP: power-limited fire alarm cable ratings: general, riser, plenum. - IDC: initiating device circuit (conventional). - Impairment: any time all or part of the system can't do its job. - Monitor module: addressable input for a dry contact. - NAC: notification appliance circuit. - NAC power extender: booster supply that adds NAC capacity. - PLFA: power-limited fire alarm circuit. - Record of Completion: NFPA 72 form documenting the installed system. - Retard: adjustable delay in a waterflow switch. - Sequence of operations: what happens when each device activates. - Shunt trip: breaker that cuts elevator power before sprinklers discharge on it. - SLC: signaling line circuit (addressable). - Supervisory: signal that fire protection equipment is off-normal. - Tamper switch: valve supervisory switch on a sprinkler control valve. - Temporal-3: the standard fire evacuation pattern. - Trouble: signal that the fire alarm system has a fault. - Waterflow switch: detects water moving in a sprinkler pipe."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"AHJ","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"Authority Having Jurisdiction; usually the fire marshal."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Alarm verification","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"the panel resets a smoke detector and rechecks before declaring an alarm."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Annunciator","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"remote display that shows what's in alarm."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Candela (cd)","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"brightness rating of a strobe."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Class A / B / X","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"how a circuit behaves with an open, ground, or short."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"DACT","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"digital alarm communicator transmitter; sends signals over phone lines."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"dBA","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"sound level, A-weighted, used for audibility."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"EVACS","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"emergency voice/alarm communication system."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"FACP / FACU","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"fire alarm control panel / unit."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Fire watch","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"people patrolling for fire while a system is impaired."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"FPL / FPLR / FPLP","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"power-limited fire alarm cable ratings: general, riser, plenum."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"IDC","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"initiating device circuit (conventional)."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Impairment","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"any time all or part of the system can't do its job."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Monitor module","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"addressable input for a dry contact."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"NAC","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"notification appliance circuit."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"NAC power extender","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"booster supply that adds NAC capacity."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"PLFA","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"power-limited fire alarm circuit."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Record of Completion","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"NFPA 72 form documenting the installed system."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Retard","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"adjustable delay in a waterflow switch."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Sequence of operations","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"what happens when each device activates."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Shunt trip","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"breaker that cuts elevator power before sprinklers discharge on it."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"SLC","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"signaling line circuit (addressable)."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Supervisory","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"signal that fire protection equipment is off-normal."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Tamper switch","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"valve supervisory switch on a sprinkler control valve."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Temporal-3","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"the standard fire evacuation pattern."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Trouble","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"signal that the fire alarm system has a fault."},{"kind":"Glossary","title":"Waterflow switch","where":"Glossary","href":"reference/glossary","text":"detects water moving in a sprinkler pipe."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Ground fault trouble","where":"Guide 1","href":"troubleshoot/1","text":"Symptom: panel shows a ground fault (sometimes with + or − polarity, sometimes with the circuit). 1. Read the panel. Does it name a circuit or module? → Start with that circuit at step 4. 2. Was anything worked on recently (remodel, new devices, roof work, rain)? → Check that area first. 3. No circuit named: disconnect field circuits one at a time (SLCs, IDCs, NACs, communicator, annunciator, power supply outputs). Wait for the panel to re-check after each one. The ground fault clears → that circuit has it. 4. With the circuit off the panel, meter each conductor to earth ground. Any reading → the fault is on that conductor. 5. Split the circuit at an accessible midpoint and test each half. Repeat until you're down to one run or device. 6. Look for: a staple or screw through the cable, wire pinched against a metal box or cover, water in an exterior or basement device, a damaged conduit fitting, or a shield drain wire touching the box. 7. No megohmmeter with devices connected. 8. Repair, reconnect, confirm the trouble clears and the circuit works. Field tip (David to add): the most common place you find a ground fault in a finished building."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Open circuit trouble (conventional IDC or NAC)","where":"Guide 2","href":"troubleshoot/2","text":"Symptom: panel shows trouble or open on a zone or NAC. 1. Look for the obvious: a device removed, a detector out of its base, a ceiling tile disturbed, recent construction. 2. Disconnect the circuit at the panel and meter it (Reference: Meter readings on fire circuits ). - Reads the EOL value → wiring is good. Check the panel terminal and the circuit's programming. Try the EOL resistor directly on the panel terminals: trouble clears → field wiring issue that comes and goes; trouble stays → panel side. - Reads open → go to step 3. 3. Find the EOL. Is it there and the right value? A missing or wrong EOL is common after another contractor's work. 4. Go device by device from the panel: check each detector is seated in its base and each terminal is tight. On two-wire smokes, a detector removed from its base opens the circuit by design. 5. Split the circuit at an accessible device and meter each half to find the break. 6. Look for a T-tap. A branch someone added will leave part of the circuit unsupervised and may hide the real break. Rewire it as a single path. 7. Reconnect, confirm the trouble clears, and test the devices past where the break was."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Addressable device missing, no answer, or wrong type","where":"Guide 3","href":"troubleshoot/3","text":"Symptom: panel shows a device missing, no answer, invalid reply, wrong device type, or duplicate address. 1. Note the address and look it up on the point list. 2. One device missing: go to it. Is it in its base? Is the base wiring tight? Is it damaged or painted over? Replace with the same type and set the same address. 3. Wrong device type: the device installed doesn't match the programming (a heat detector where a smoke is programmed). Install the correct type or have the program changed by someone authorized, per the drawings. 4. Duplicate address: two devices set to the same address. Common after a device swap. Find both and correct one. 5. Many devices missing past one point: an open on the SLC (Class B), a short isolated by an isolator (Class X), or a damaged cable. Go to the first missing device and check the wiring in and out. 6. Devices dropping in and out: SLC wiring too long, too much capacitance, unshielded cable near noise, a loose splice, or a ground fault. Check the panel's SLC limits and the loop's wiring. 7. After repair, confirm the device reports at the right address with the right label, and test it."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Horns or strobes not working, or NAC trouble","where":"Guide 4","href":"troubleshoot/4","text":"Symptom: NAC trouble on the panel, or some appliances don't sound or flash during a test. 1. NAC trouble with no alarm: treat as an open or short (Guide 2 for opens). A short on a NAC usually shows when the panel tries to activate it; meter both directions for a short. 2. Some appliances work, some don't: - All the dead ones past one point → open or bad splice between the last working and first dead appliance. - Random dead ones → bad appliance, appliance wired backward (polarity), or a loose terminal. 3. Strobes dim, slow, or not flashing at the far end: voltage drop. Measure voltage at the last appliance during alarm . Compare to the appliance's minimum (commonly 16 V). Use the NAC Voltage Drop calculator to confirm and fix (heavier wire, split the circuit, or add an extender). 4. Strobes flashing out of sync: sync protocol mismatch between panel, extender, or appliance brands; an extender not set to follow the panel; or mixed appliance models. 5. NAC extender circuits dead: check the extender's AC, batteries, and trouble LEDs, and the trigger wiring from the panel. 6. Panel shuts down the NAC: total current over the NAC rating. Add up every appliance at its setting. 7. Retest every appliance on the circuit after the fix."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"AC power loss or battery trouble","where":"Guide 5","href":"troubleshoot/5","text":"Symptom: panel shows AC loss, low battery, battery trouble, or charger trouble. 1. AC loss: check the fire alarm breaker (it should be red-marked and locked). Tripped or turned off? → Find out why before resetting. Then check the panel's AC fuse and terminal connections. 2. AC good but low battery: measure battery voltage with the batteries on the charger, then under load (disconnect AC for a short test, or use a battery load tester). - Low with AC on for more than a couple of days → charger problem or a dead battery. - Good with no load, collapses under load → battery is failing. Replace both. 3. Check the date on the batteries. Older than about 5 years → replace both. 4. Battery trouble / missing: loose lead, blown battery fuse, corroded terminals, or a jumper missing between the two batteries. 5. Batteries keep dying: run a battery calculation (Fire Battery calculator) against actual current. Too much load for the battery size, or a battery bigger than the charger can recharge. 6. Date the new batteries and confirm the trouble clears."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Nuisance smoke alarms","where":"Guide 6","href":"troubleshoot/6","text":"Symptom: repeated alarms from a smoke detector with no fire. 1. Pull the history: same device each time? Same time of day? (Cooking, cleaning, HVAC cycling, shift change, sprinkler testing.) 2. Addressable: check the detector's sensitivity and dirty or maintenance alert on the panel. Dirty → clean per manufacturer or replace. 3. Look at the environment: steam (showers, kitchens), dust (construction, warehouses), insects, exhaust, aerosol sprays, humidity, or temperature outside the listed range. 4. Check the location: within 3 ft of a supply diffuser or return? Air drawing dust into it or blowing across it? 5. Construction dust? Clean or replace, and make sure dust covers are used next time. 6. Fix the cause: clean, replace, or relocate or change the detector type with the designer's or AHJ's approval . Don't change device types on your own. 7. Never leave a nuisance detector disabled without notifying the owner and AHJ and documenting the impairment. Field tip (David to add): a nuisance alarm cause that took you a long time to find."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Supervisory won't clear (tamper, duct, or other)","where":"Guide 7","href":"troubleshoot/7","text":"Symptom: supervisory signal on the panel that stays after a reset. 1. Identify the device. Is it a valve tamper, duct detector, low air, fire pump, or other monitored input? 2. Valve tamper: is the valve fully open? Coordinate with the building or sprinkler contractor; never open or close sprinkler valves yourself unless you're authorized. A partly closed valve won't restore. 3. Valve fully open but still supervisory → adjust or replace the switch; check that the switch's trip lever sits correctly on the valve. 4. Duct detector: check the detector's LED and the remote test station. Dirty, in alarm, or in trouble? Check airflow across the sampling tubes. Reset at the detector if it latches locally. (Guide 10) 5. Wiring: meter the circuit. A short on a supervisory input can read as an active supervisory. 6. Reset the panel and confirm the supervisory clears."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Waterflow alarm problems","where":"Guide 8","href":"troubleshoot/8","text":"Symptom: waterflow alarm with no fire, or no alarm when water flows. 1. False waterflow alarms: check for pressure surges (fire pump starting, city water pressure changes). Check the retard setting; it may be too short. Don't set it so long that it exceeds the 90-second requirement. 2. Is water actually moving? A leak, a broken head, or an open drain will cause a real waterflow. Investigate before you call it false. 3. Check the switch for corrosion, water in the electrical housing, or a damaged paddle. 4. No alarm on flow: test with the inspector's test valve with the sprinkler contractor. Time it. No signal → check the switch contact with a meter while water flows, then the wiring and module or zone. 5. Confirm the waterflow is not on the same zone as a tamper switch."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Failure to communicate with the monitoring center","where":"Guide 9","href":"troubleshoot/9","text":"Symptom: panel shows communication trouble, or the monitoring center isn't receiving signals or periodic tests. 1. Which path failed? (Phone line 1 or 2, cellular, IP.) 2. Phone line (DACT): is there dial tone at the panel? Lines often get disconnected or moved to VoIP without anyone telling the alarm company. VoIP may not pass DACT signals reliably. 3. Cellular: check signal strength at the communicator, the antenna connection, and that the account is active with the provider. 4. IP: check the network cable, the switch port, and that the network still allows the communicator out. 5. Check the account number and receiver numbers in the panel or communicator programming. 6. Send a test signal and confirm with the operator that the account and event came in correctly. 7. Until the path is restored, the system is impaired for off-site reporting; notify the owner."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Panel won't reset","where":"Guide 10","href":"troubleshoot/10","text":"Symptom: the panel goes back into alarm, supervisory, or trouble right after a reset. 1. Which device is still active? Find it on the panel. 2. Pull station: still pulled down. Reset it with its key or tool. 3. Smoke detector: smoke or dust still in the chamber. Wait, clear it, or replace it. On a four-wire zone, the panel must drop resettable power to reset the detector; check the resettable power output and the EOL relay. 4. Duct detector: some latch at the detector and need a local reset or a reset from the remote test station. 5. Heat detector: a non-restorable head has fused and must be replaced. 6. Waterflow: water is still flowing. 7. Monitor module input still closed: check the contact it watches (a suppression panel, fire pump controller, etc.). 8. Short on an IDC looks like a device in alarm on many conventional panels. Disconnect the zone and meter it (Guide 2)."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Elevator recall or control function didn't operate","where":"Guide 11","href":"troubleshoot/11","text":"Symptom: during a test, elevators didn't recall, fans didn't shut down, or doors didn't release. 1. Was the output disabled for testing and not re-enabled? Check the panel's disabled list first. 2. Check the relay or control module: does it change state when the input is activated? Listen and meter the contact. 3. Check the wiring from the relay to the other trade's equipment. The relay should be within 3 ft of the controlled device with a supervised circuit to it. 4. Check the programming against the sequence of operations: is the input mapped to that output? 5. Elevators: confirm lobby detector, machine room, and hoistway signals go to the right recall input (primary or alternate floor). Test with the elevator contractor present. 6. Retest the full sequence after the fix and document it."},{"kind":"Troubleshoot","title":"Detector or device needs replacing","where":"Guide 12","href":"troubleshoot/12","text":"Symptom: a detector fails sensitivity, is damaged, or is past its service life. 1. Get the exact replacement: same type, listed and compatible with the panel. A different model may need panel programming or may not be compatible at all. 2. Put the account on test and disable the device or zone. 3. Replace the head (or base and head), set the same address on addressable systems. 4. Confirm the panel sees the correct device type at the correct address, with no troubles. 5. Test the new device with listed smoke or the correct method. 6. Record the replacement on the test report."}]});
